[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537889 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2500f223-2c89-5c0f-a8ea-dd1a400d8458" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537308}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2500f223-2c89-5c0f-a8ea-dd1a400d8458" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537308} :answer-field/id #uuid "08ffe6b8-fc77-5a91-a8ee-c6dda37b1ac2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "199584af-715c-57d8-b77d-cfea8258f652" :question/math-academy-id "q-250510" :question/problem "If the function $f(x)=3{log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{x-1}{3})-k?$ has an inverse function given by\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=3\\cdot {2}^{(x+6)/3}+1,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=3{log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{x-1}{3})-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=3{log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{y-1}{3})-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3{log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{y-1}{3})-k \\\\ 3{log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{y-1}{3}) & =x+k \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{y-1}{3}) & =\\frac{x+k}{3} \\\\ {2}^{{log}_{2}⁡((y-1)/3)} & ={2}^{(x+k)/3} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{3} & ={2}^{(x+k)/3} \\\\ y-1 & =3\\cdot {2}^{(x+k)/3} \\\\ y & =3\\cdot {2}^{(x+k)/3}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=3\\cdot {2}^{(x+k)/3}+1.$ \n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=6.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537892 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2359ac3e-05f6-59cf-b866-9b382c762ab9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537304}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2359ac3e-05f6-59cf-b866-9b382c762ab9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537304} :answer-field/id #uuid "72f2aba8-3ad3-5527-af94-606d696f3adf" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537806} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7db4dc2-4afa-5530-8d81-ba6a2b0a54c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7db4dc2-4afa-5530-8d81-ba6a2b0a54c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537585} :answer-field/id #uuid "bc429605-87f5-544d-978a-0285dffc3203" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537850} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de0c8485-931d-5e14-9d0b-1416b5ec8693" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537676}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de0c8485-931d-5e14-9d0b-1416b5ec8693" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537676} :answer-field/id #uuid "df5421c1-33c8-52c3-a934-b3f293d407a7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1ebb4c16-c258-518a-a825-ce276a761a9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-351898" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}({cosh}^{2}⁡(\\frac{3x}{2})+1)\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sinh}⁡($\n{{field-2}}\n$)+\\,$\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -1\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta =2{cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta -1\n$$\n\n \n\nRearranging, we have\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta +1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{3x}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡(\\frac{3x}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡3x+1).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}({cosh}^{2}⁡(\\frac{3x}{2})+1)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡3x+1)+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡3x+\\frac{3}{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x+\\frac{3}{2}x)|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})+\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}]-[\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡0+0] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\,\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})+\\frac{3\\pi }{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537896 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11623763-75f4-582c-8cf1-89865dc45e28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537258} {:answer/id #uuid "173a6cfb-d807-5412-8e19-23721709e4c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537277} {:answer/id #uuid "60e2afc2-23df-527e-8d4d-06bcc3f5634f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537420} {:answer/id #uuid "f8d10f1c-8d35-5c14-a45c-90112e2423c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537739} {:answer/id #uuid "f9d7fe5d-6a2b-5e6d-aa5b-be15665a7768" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537744}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "173a6cfb-d807-5412-8e19-23721709e4c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537277} :answer-field/id #uuid "7dd89537-63c4-5254-bd6d-b7533a181887" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537810}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2340627e-913d-5c02-af0a-4af27f0e0758" :question/math-academy-id "q-148367" :question/problem "The function $f(x)=\\frac{2}{3}-{5}^{2x-k}$ has an inverse function given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{2-3x}{3}).\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{2}{3}-{5}^{2x-k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{3}-{5}^{2y-k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{3}-{5}^{2y-k} \\\\ {5}^{2y-k} & =\\frac{2}{3}-x \\\\ {5}^{2y-k} & =\\frac{2-3x}{3} \\\\ {log}_{5}⁡({5}^{2y-k}) & ={log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{2-3x}{3}) \\\\ 2y-k & ={log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{2-3x}{3}) \\\\ 2y & ={log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{2-3x}{3})+k \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{2-3x}{3})+\\frac{k}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{2-3x}{3})+\\frac{k}{2}$  \n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537905 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f374302c-dbd0-5652-afd7-79c66b75d75d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537730}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f374302c-dbd0-5652-afd7-79c66b75d75d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537730} :answer-field/id #uuid "075d7fee-b2d5-5957-a28d-a7eec528b93a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3b5ffb6f-7067-559d-aec0-81e9ef0a06b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-250507" :question/problem "If the function $f(x)={log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{x-1}{5})-k$ has an inverse function given by\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=5\\cdot {2}^{x-3}+1,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{x-1}{5})-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{y-1}{5})-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{y-1}{5})-k \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{y-1}{5}) & =x+k \\\\ {2}^{{log}_{2}⁡((y-1)/5)} & ={2}^{x+k} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{5} & ={2}^{x+k} \\\\ y-1 & =5\\cdot {2}^{x+k} \\\\ y & =5\\cdot {2}^{x+k}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=5\\cdot {2}^{x+k}+1.$ \n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=-3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537909 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a853eb43-35ad-5318-b3ea-fd258865a714" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537546}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a853eb43-35ad-5318-b3ea-fd258865a714" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537546} :answer-field/id #uuid "98cc11c0-aee2-5fda-a252-3022cc314d32" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537825} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ab22a23-f9cc-56d5-95ac-279199c70b5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537436}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ab22a23-f9cc-56d5-95ac-279199c70b5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537436} :answer-field/id #uuid "b45263fa-64ca-5fca-a711-c9b1f2ec9306" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537842} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0395874-ffc8-59ad-a520-d723c8af1b40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537532}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0395874-ffc8-59ad-a520-d723c8af1b40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537532} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0ee657f-8a91-5462-a83e-e961791c1d5e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537866}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4b85bc81-6104-5328-b444-83c8973655d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-352792" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}(8{sinh}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})-4)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{sinh}⁡($\n{{field-2}}\n$)-\\,$\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta =1-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta =1+2{sinh}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n{sinh}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2\\theta -1).\n$$\n\nLetting $\\theta =\\frac{x}{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n{sinh}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-1).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(8{sinh}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2})-4)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-1)-4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x-8)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x-8x)\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(1)-8\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537938 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1df39945-f17e-5051-9124-626d55bf8a1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537293} {:answer/id #uuid "64742e1b-bec8-5682-b5ee-105f5d4f7a68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537429} {:answer/id #uuid "7532c4d0-18e0-53bb-a6b7-c9f7c5d650eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537461} {:answer/id #uuid "abfebc27-8159-524d-9a6e-cdfd59eb0187" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537553} {:answer/id #uuid "befbf0c7-7d19-5ffd-943e-139e02963d23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1df39945-f17e-5051-9124-626d55bf8a1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537293} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8e3c1c2-59cd-57ee-a592-7d15bb5c605d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "86933fa6-f8f7-5354-b325-40dd23c73f55" :question/math-academy-id "q-75921" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where $\\frac{x-2}{2x}+\\frac{x-4}{2x}=\\frac{5x-6}{2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that $x=0$ is an excluded solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nWe multiply both sides of the equation by $2x,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{2x}+\\frac{x-4}{2x} & =\\frac{5x-6}{2x} \\\\ 2x\\cdot (\\frac{x-2}{2x}+\\frac{x-4}{2x}) & =n\\cdot (\\frac{5x-6}{2x}) \\\\ 2x\\cdot \\frac{x-2}{2x}+2x\\cdot \\frac{x-4}{2x} & =2x\\cdot \\frac{5x-6}{2x} \\\\ \\cancel{2x}\\cdot \\frac{x-2}{\\cancel{2x}}+\\cancel{2x}\\cdot \\frac{x-4}{\\cancel{2x}} & =\\cancel{2x}\\cdot \\frac{5x-6}{\\cancel{2x}} \\\\ x-2+x-4 & =5x-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2+x-4 & =5x-6 \\\\ 2x-6 & =5x-6 \\\\ 5x-2x & =-6+6 \\\\ 3x & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. Therefore, we conclude that there is no solution."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537940 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20e1836d-75eb-5de8-9c9c-af521731af67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{10}^{x}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537301} {:answer/id #uuid "338596eb-3c1b-5bb8-a994-1d2cbd79a6de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={10}^{x/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537338} {:answer/id #uuid "a68559e7-d056-5e43-81d2-70700c2a99e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=3\\cdot {10}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537543} {:answer/id #uuid "b4ad9448-aed0-5988-be94-5a5e58fa5d2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={10}^{x}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537578} {:answer/id #uuid "cdffd6c4-c3d8-5c8f-9f1a-ca2d437d5ed7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)={10}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537631}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a68559e7-d056-5e43-81d2-70700c2a99e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=3\\cdot {10}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537543} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ff5f77d-c752-52bb-b6ec-a317055ea25e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537770}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9b1ff915-f7df-590e-9573-48667059c317" :question/math-academy-id "q-46621" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}).$" :question/worked-solution "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{y}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{y}{3}) \\\\ {10}^{x} & ={10}^{\\operatorname{log}⁡(y/3)} \\\\ {10}^{x} & =\\frac{y}{3} \\\\ y & =3\\cdot {10}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=3\\cdot {10}^{x}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537947 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f05b0eb8-1f64-572b-9eb7-f009dba2ee76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8x+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537724}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f05b0eb8-1f64-572b-9eb7-f009dba2ee76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8x+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537724} :answer-field/id #uuid "a93c79b4-53dc-5d15-9856-ca96c8afc783" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a35127e9-e5f3-508e-9d5a-b3279aa78b1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-340405" :question/problem "Use the definition of the derivative to find ${f}^{′}(x)$ if  $f(x)=-4{x}^{2}+5x.$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The definition of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)={lim}_{h\\to 0}\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying this to $f(x)=-4{x}^{2}+5x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{-4(x+h)^{2}+5(x+h)-(-4{x}^{2}+5x)}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{-4({x}^{2}+2xh+{h}^{2})+5x+5h+4{x}^{2}-5x}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{-4{x}^{2}-8xh-4{h}^{2}+5x+5h+4{x}^{2}-5x}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{-8xh-4{h}^{2}+5h}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{h(-8x-4h+5)}{h}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{h\\to 0}(-8x-4h+5) \\\\  & =-8x+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537950 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "027489bb-28e3-5dfe-a2b6-367b579e0221" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537229} {:answer/id #uuid "2ceb2f6f-5d34-5be3-a06c-024dd0503854" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537323} {:answer/id #uuid "2e591bdc-0ce1-5740-b9d1-67306bc53ff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537327} {:answer/id #uuid "8090692a-b362-571b-91a3-0212386b7f7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537479} {:answer/id #uuid "fb8501be-4fcb-577e-8819-117055fdb0d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537749}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e591bdc-0ce1-5740-b9d1-67306bc53ff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537327} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e75216d-bcd6-569a-b621-c249dcbf26bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537765}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a581df95-2d51-59ad-a71c-d2138e307e87" :question/math-academy-id "q-3518" :question/problem "Solve for $x$ where $\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{1}{x}=\\frac{3-x}{3x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined.\n\nThe equation has the denominators $x$ and $3x.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(x,3x)=3x.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $3x,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x\\cdot (\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{1}{x}) & =3x\\cdot (\\frac{3-x}{3x}) \\\\ 3x\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}+3x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x} & =3x\\cdot \\frac{3-x}{3x} \\\\ \\cancel{3}x\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\cancel{3}}+3\\cancel{x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\cancel{x}} & =\\cancel{3x}\\cdot \\frac{3-x}{\\cancel{3x}} \\\\ x\\cdot (2)+3\\cdot (1) & =3-x \\\\ 2x+3 & =3-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+3 & =3-x \\\\ 2x+x & =3-3 \\\\ 3x & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. Since $x=0,$ we conclude that there is no solution."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537953 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31269d34-bf9b-5beb-8795-1803e3e8f873" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{(x+h)-(2x+1)}{h})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537335} {:answer/id #uuid "7ab95eba-e6f1-50b3-93cf-0ac8c24f9109" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{2(x+h)+1-(2x+1)}{h})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537468} {:answer/id #uuid "957167b9-3ca4-5394-9fd5-5962be3124cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{(x+h)+x}{h})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537512} {:answer/id #uuid "b02f15e2-ec30-5b7e-8dd6-d998fa88fd43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{2x+1-(x+h+1)}{h})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537562} {:answer/id #uuid "daeffdac-cd8f-5ed5-9255-0868e62e563e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{2x+1-(x+h)}{h})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537667}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ab95eba-e6f1-50b3-93cf-0ac8c24f9109" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{2(x+h)+1-(2x+1)}{h})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537468} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4d0ff0d-7860-5a8a-a6e6-4fb8f62ae866" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537874}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ab9ad28a-3195-5102-ad22-90c2dc511297" :question/math-academy-id "q-101957" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=2x+1$, what is ${f}^{′}(x)$ according to the definition of the derivative?" :question/worked-solution "The derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ is given by the limit \n\n$$\n{lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}).\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)=2x+1$,  we have that $f(x+h)=2(x+h)+1.$ Hence,  we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={lim}_{h\\to 0}(\\frac{2(x+h)+1-(2x+1)}{h}).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537971 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75c584e3-4715-56f3-957f-04a6776e7cb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\ln(3)-\\frac{40}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537464}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75c584e3-4715-56f3-957f-04a6776e7cb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\ln(3)-\\frac{40}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537464} :answer-field/id #uuid "b73b1863-d6f0-5b10-b3fa-815bfcf7b139" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537846}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d0857a55-78b2-5e38-b682-a21c51974c3c" :question/math-academy-id "q-352425" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round your answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(2-12{sinh}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the following double-angle formula for cosine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x=1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, we get the following double-angle formula for the hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x=1+2{sinh}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nWe can rearrange the above formula as\n\n$$\n{sinh}^{2}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x-1).\n$$\n\nWe can now integrate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(2-12{sinh}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}[2-12\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x-1)]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(8-6\\operatorname{cosh}⁡2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(8x-3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x)\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =[8\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3)]-[0-0] \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-3(\\frac{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-{e}^{-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}}{2}) \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-3(\\frac{{3}^{2}-{3}^{-2}}{2}) \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{ln}⁡3-\\frac{40}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537984 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "173e1c1a-757c-5fcf-be6a-91e7ca4875db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537278} {:answer/id #uuid "637488d7-0f63-5fe0-8698-1d6414fe8f0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537427} {:answer/id #uuid "b33107cc-21e0-55e3-a66c-7183491e915e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537576} {:answer/id #uuid "f109c179-6843-5d73-a8d7-5f145c4e38c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537726} {:answer/id #uuid "ff7a5d0d-e5eb-5ce1-ad1a-caa3023a2a5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537755}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f109c179-6843-5d73-a8d7-5f145c4e38c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537726} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b11927f-a39d-501b-8f1e-1dbd9842d17d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f095dc29-06a2-59bf-8997-6e524951d317" :question/math-academy-id "q-75936" :question/problem "Which value of $m$ satisfies the equation $\\frac{4m+1}{3m}-\\frac{m-1}{6m}=\\frac{1}{9m}?$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $m=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $3m,6m,$ and $9m.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(3m,6m,9m)=18m.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $18m,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4m+1}{3m}-\\frac{m-1}{6m} & =\\frac{1}{9m} \\\\ 18m\\cdot (\\frac{4m+1}{3m}-\\frac{m-1}{6m}) & =18m\\cdot (\\frac{1}{9m}) \\\\ 18m\\cdot (\\frac{4m+1}{3m})-18m\\cdot (\\frac{m-1}{6m}) & =18m\\cdot (\\frac{1}{9m}) \\\\ 6\\cdot 3m\\cdot (\\frac{4m+1}{3m})-3\\cdot 6m\\cdot (\\frac{m-1}{6m}) & =2\\cdot 9m\\cdot (\\frac{1}{9m}) \\\\ 6\\cdot \\cancel{3m}\\cdot (\\frac{4m+1}{\\cancel{3m}})-3\\cdot \\cancel{6m}\\cdot (\\frac{m-1}{\\cancel{6m}}) & =2\\cdot \\cancel{9m}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\cancel{9m}}) \\\\ 6(4m+1)-3(m-1) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $m,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(4m+1)-3(m-1) & =2 \\\\ 24m+6-3m+3 & =2 \\\\ 21m+9 & =2 \\\\ 21m & =-7 \\\\ m & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $m=0$ as a solution. But since $m=-\\frac{1}{3}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $m=-\\frac{1}{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537988 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b7a2e99-60fa-5845-b485-7f1ecf41d0a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537243} {:answer/id #uuid "2db04f8a-deb3-5e11-91d5-eea128cb749d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537326} {:answer/id #uuid "b61f0de4-08b9-5e89-9aaa-a012a63e7f6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537581} {:answer/id #uuid "c71bf5dc-237a-5908-9987-a1df036cb19a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537615} {:answer/id #uuid "cf71e830-8356-5e8d-bce8-f9b93f8cc125" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537635}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2db04f8a-deb3-5e11-91d5-eea128cb749d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537326} :answer-field/id #uuid "efc35caa-71e9-53b2-85d0-1e3c73992105" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537871}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f7541a79-898f-52f6-9741-385d5ef8e2c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-75955" :question/problem "Which value of $n$ satisfies the equation $\\frac{3}{2n}-\\frac{1-n}{{n}^{2}}=\\frac{7}{3n}?$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $n=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $2n,$ ${n}^{2},$ and $3n.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(2n,{n}^{2},3n)=6{n}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $6{n}^{2},$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2n}-\\frac{1-n}{{n}^{2}} & =\\frac{7}{3n} \\\\ 6{n}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2n}-\\frac{1-n}{{n}^{2}}) & =6{n}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{7}{3n}) \\\\ 6{n}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2n})-6{n}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1-n}{{n}^{2}}) & =6{n}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{7}{3n}) \\\\ 3n\\cdot 2n\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2n})-6\\cdot {n}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1-n}{{n}^{2}}) & =2n\\cdot 3n\\cdot (\\frac{7}{3n}) \\\\ 3n\\cdot \\cancel{2n}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{\\cancel{2n}})-6\\cdot \\cancel{{n}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{1-n}{\\cancel{{n}^{2}}}) & =2n\\cdot \\cancel{3n}\\cdot (\\frac{7}{\\cancel{3n}}) \\\\ 3n\\cdot (3)-6\\cdot (1-n) & =2n\\cdot (7) \\\\ 9n-6(1-n) & =14n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $n,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9n-6(1-n) & =14n \\\\ 15n-6 & =14n \\\\ n & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $n=0$ as a solution. But since $n=6\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $n=6.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537989 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ce934f1-749e-5c82-9be2-eb4043940d41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537322} {:answer/id #uuid "3fb68e51-5ee4-5bd9-86b7-657995c28427" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537360} {:answer/id #uuid "6248e345-a77f-5d0b-a6d9-29b049db316b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537425} {:answer/id #uuid "b2172526-cf2e-5a91-b2a6-d166613ff60b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537574} {:answer/id #uuid "dc07d439-b7d4-549a-97f7-0b5dc5b32343" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6248e345-a77f-5d0b-a6d9-29b049db316b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537425} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f7254ba-8516-55b4-929d-3effa926b080" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537831}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fa991cba-7607-5ad7-99b3-e6c6f2993b9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-115795" :question/problem "Solve for $q$ where $\\frac{2q-1}{4q}=\\frac{q+1}{3q}-1.$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $q=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $3q$ and $4q.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(3q,4q)=12q.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $12q,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2q-1}{4q} & =\\frac{q+1}{3q}-1 \\\\ 12q\\cdot (\\frac{2q-1}{4q}) & =12q\\cdot (\\frac{q+1}{3q}-1) \\\\ 12q\\cdot \\frac{2q-1}{4q} & =12q\\cdot \\frac{q+1}{3q}-12q\\cdot 1 \\\\ 3\\cdot \\cancel{4q}\\cdot (\\frac{2q-1}{\\cancel{4q}}) & =4\\cdot \\cancel{3q}\\cdot (\\frac{q+1}{\\cancel{3q}})-12q \\\\ 3(2q-1) & =4(q+1)-12q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $q,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(2q-1) & =4(q+1)-12q \\\\ 6q-3 & =4q+4-12q \\\\ 6q-3 & =-8q+4 \\\\ 6q+8q & =4+3 \\\\ 14q & =7 \\\\ q & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $q=0$ as a solution. But since $q=\\frac{1}{2}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $q=\\frac{1}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537992 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38875fd2-c051-51ae-9cd4-a572e31fc61c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537344}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38875fd2-c051-51ae-9cd4-a572e31fc61c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537344} :answer-field/id #uuid "787aa0da-04c6-5397-99c2-28c45379391c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537808}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ff75b330-ecd4-5fa0-b36d-564d6c66e864" :question/math-academy-id "q-276869" :question/problem "Solve for $v$ where $\\frac{v+4}{{v}^{2}}-\\frac{7}{6v}=\\frac{5}{2{v}^{2}}.$\n\n$v=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We note that $v=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $6v,\\,{v}^{2},$ and $2{v}^{2}.$ First, we find the denominators' least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$). We have that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(6v,{v}^{2},2{v}^{2})=6{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $6{v}^{2},$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{v+4}{{v}^{2}}-\\frac{7}{6v} & =\\frac{5}{2{v}^{2}} \\\\ 6{v}^{2}(\\frac{v+4}{{v}^{2}}-\\frac{7}{6v}) & =6{v}^{2}(\\frac{5}{2{v}^{2}}) \\\\ 6{v}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{v+4}{{v}^{2}}-6{v}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{7}{6v} & =6{v}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2{v}^{2}} \\\\ 6\\cdot {v}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{v+4}{{v}^{2}}-6v\\cdot v\\cdot \\frac{7}{6v} & =3\\cdot 2{v}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{2{v}^{2}} \\\\ 6\\cdot \\cancel{{v}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{v+4}{\\cancel{{v}^{2}}}-\\cancel{6v}\\cdot v\\cdot \\frac{7}{\\cancel{6v}} & =3\\cdot \\cancel{2{v}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{5}{\\cancel{2{v}^{2}}} \\\\ 6(v+4)-v\\cdot (7) & =3\\cdot 5 \\\\ 6(v+4)-7v & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $v,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6(v+4)-7v & =15 \\\\ 6v+24-7v & =15 \\\\ -v+24 & =15 \\\\ -v & =-9 \\\\ v & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $v=0$ as a solution. But since $v=9\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $v=9.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538929 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03ad4804-5d67-5f61-a2ef-fb8a13a792b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538241} {:answer/id #uuid "3957d0a7-b17e-59fa-ba9f-5a550a649663" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64\\cdot {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538351} {:answer/id #uuid "775ad280-4252-584a-9cd3-1acf0234c161" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{64}^{6}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538502} {:answer/id #uuid "b41a5b18-1ac7-5ab7-a7f0-296fa3f34e5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{64}^{6}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538652} {:answer/id #uuid "ef88dff2-c156-5cd2-98da-be865d390a62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64\\cdot {(2)}^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538777}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03ad4804-5d67-5f61-a2ef-fb8a13a792b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538241} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0410c69-ce09-548b-ba42-743ef4a478a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538889}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "11af995c-4c5e-50de-8182-ca4e90fd1ecf" :question/math-academy-id "q-61965" :question/problem "A soccer tournament began with $64$ teams. The number of teams reduced by $\\frac{1}{2}$ after every round. How many teams remained after $6$ rounds?" :question/worked-solution "There were $64$ initially, and this number decreased by $\\frac{1}{2}$ every round. \n\nTherefore:\n\n- After $1$ round, there were $64\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2})$ teams remaining.\n\n- After $2$ rounds, there were $64\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}$ teams remaining.\n\n- After $3$ rounds, there were $64\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}$ teams  remaining.\n\n- $…$\n\nRepeating this pattern, we see that there were $64\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{6}$ teams remaining after $6$ rounds."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538935 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "440832e3-ec7e-581c-830b-fa0617454e21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538370} {:answer/id #uuid "582dfc2f-ec43-56ae-b0da-a85459f5f647" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538419} {:answer/id #uuid "6ce9db67-a4d8-528a-ab5f-3678c39d003b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538477} {:answer/id #uuid "8f9bda09-f1d7-582f-8b9e-ab2ac1effc46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538570} {:answer/id #uuid "cf97a1ce-c65c-5f65-82ac-6f5c300dc9ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538713}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf97a1ce-c65c-5f65-82ac-6f5c300dc9ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538713} :answer-field/id #uuid "30f439c6-850c-556a-a1db-f9a9d3a4a19c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538829}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "224b72f4-7dda-5cd7-89ac-9950a44cc628" :question/math-academy-id "q-132437" :question/problem "Given that $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}),$ what is $\\text{Var}[X]?$" :question/worked-solution "If $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\lambda )$ is an exponential random variable, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\frac{1}{{\\lambda }^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for our random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}),$ we have the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\frac{1}{{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{3}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538937 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e08ccbd4-8902-537c-bf90-b675fffaa98d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538746}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e08ccbd4-8902-537c-bf90-b675fffaa98d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538746} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffbb06e8-1787-59c8-b541-824a6fbe128b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538924}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2c9f2909-7e69-5612-95f1-1f03b540b4de" :question/math-academy-id "q-175591" :question/problem "If $-\\frac{x}{12}=-\\frac{1}{6},$ then $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's remove the negative sign from the variable. To do this, we multiply both sides of the equation by $-1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{x}{12} & =-\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{12}) & =(-1)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\ \\frac{x}{12} & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the equation above, $x$ is being divided by $12.$ To isolate $x$, we can perform the opposite operation, which is *multiplying* by $12.$ So, we multiply both sides of the equation by $12$ and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{12} & =\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ 12\\cdot \\frac{x}{12} & =12\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\ \\frac{12x}{12} & =\\frac{12}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{12}x}{\\cancel{12}} & =2 \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538944 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2840fb0c-b24c-5ace-8c60-e5f1d39630ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.1\\,\\text{in}/\\text{h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538315} {:answer/id #uuid "5278ff3e-cb2e-5401-af52-b131b740deb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.1\\,\\text{in}/\\text{h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538410} {:answer/id #uuid "7aa711cb-bd92-5be9-85c2-c50e7b5fcea3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13.5\\,\\text{in}/\\text{h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538512} {:answer/id #uuid "9b96d177-b725-5a00-a09c-c5f9e4a3f622" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14.7\\,\\text{in}/\\text{h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538600} {:answer/id #uuid "f476b44e-8168-549a-91d7-1e7d0fc87e75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15.9\\,\\text{in}/\\text{h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538785}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2840fb0c-b24c-5ace-8c60-e5f1d39630ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12.1\\,\\text{in}/\\text{h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538315} :answer-field/id #uuid "269b10db-2af0-5890-bf3b-16469d2146ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538822}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "371ac971-2ba8-5a04-9b8d-bbb2a2347b46" :question/math-academy-id "q-121678" :question/problem "![](images/b1/b18b1e3a686f70a4c583324ddc253e8d334f63f017639bd47f6f483d42582801.png)\n\nAn inverted conical dispenser has a diameter of $100\\,\\text{in}$ and a depth of $110\\,\\text{in}.$ If a liquid is flowing out of the vertex of container at a rate of $9\\,000\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}\\text{/h}$, how fast is the height of the liquid dropping when the liquid is $60\\,\\text{in}$ deep?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the dimensions of the container. Let's call $H=110\\,\\text{in}$ the height of the container, and $R=\\frac{100}{2}=50\\,\\text{in}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the water level. We call $h$ the water level's height and $r$ the radius of the cone formed by the water. Both of these quantities, and the water volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-9\\,000\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{h},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=60\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $h$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $v.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the container and the water must be similar. Therefore, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{50}{110} \\\\ r & =\\frac{5h}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{5h}{11})}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{363}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{25}{363}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{25}{121}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we plug in the known values, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-9\\,000\\pi  & =\\frac{25}{121}\\pi (60)^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ -9\\,000\\pi  & =\\frac{90\\,000}{121}\\pi \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-12.1\\,\\text{in}/\\text{h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the water height is decreasing at a rate of $12.1\\,\\text{in}/\\text{h}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538945 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "725300ba-c2fd-5ef3-8e8b-23e9b44e688b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "725300ba-c2fd-5ef3-8e8b-23e9b44e688b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538489} :answer-field/id #uuid "8dea3e67-c006-5eec-a9a7-cb2408ce3d5f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538874}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "38c8627e-3005-599a-a37a-fb4e0aa52cad" :question/math-academy-id "q-350677" :question/problem "What is the sum of the series $1-\\frac{{(3\\pi /2)}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{(3\\pi /2)}^{4}}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,(3\\pi /2)^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯\\,?$\n\nThe sum of the series is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We recognize that the series looks similar to the Maclaurin expansion for $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,{x}^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ into our Maclaurin expansion gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) & =1-\\frac{{(3\\pi /2)}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{(3\\pi /2)}^{4}}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,(3\\pi /2)^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯ \\\\ 0 & =1-\\frac{{(3\\pi /2)}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{(3\\pi /2)}^{4}}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,(3\\pi /2)^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the sum of the series is $0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538946 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34b6e35c-dea1-538c-98c8-0094d3f2c403" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538338} {:answer/id #uuid "571409cc-60e9-5612-9926-6ed9afcda997" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{1}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538417} {:answer/id #uuid "8d889743-ce2a-5980-a928-bf56bdaac70d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538562} {:answer/id #uuid "8f0d5014-3b13-5aea-b08f-1fb39fc462a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538567} {:answer/id #uuid "ea987183-87c9-5540-a30a-3f341eb3b346" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea987183-87c9-5540-a30a-3f341eb3b346" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538768} :answer-field/id #uuid "2786ba38-c706-503a-8183-84ee0ea15a87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538824}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3922f3d0-9ec0-55ed-b2ff-7790813baa5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-103114" :question/problem "$1-\\frac{25}{2!}+\\frac{625}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,{5}^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯=$" :question/worked-solution "We recognize that the series looks similar to the Maclaurin expansion for $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,{x}^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=5$ into our Maclaurin expansion gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡5 & =1-\\frac{{5}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{5}^{4}}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,{5}^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯ \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡5 & =1-\\frac{25}{2!}+\\frac{625}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,{5}^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the sum of the series is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡5.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538951 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "371b1475-55c5-532f-b34a-de18250ba6b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No real solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538346} {:answer/id #uuid "63b5b7ee-a0bf-5cce-8cd4-ef0e8b697d72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{log}⁡2-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538454} {:answer/id #uuid "792df15e-6b0d-5e4d-9e48-244de04a293b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538507} {:answer/id #uuid "cd4beebd-bb27-5b86-b030-66ca40a0d99e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538707} {:answer/id #uuid "dd22f80c-0bb5-52dd-af6a-2e0500564142" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{5}{2})+\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538740}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "371b1475-55c5-532f-b34a-de18250ba6b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No real solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538346} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9bc71c7-9b59-5c65-af5a-72086d309520" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538894}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "41f4ba90-bf6f-5f13-ae98-77405268cba9" :question/math-academy-id "q-27725" :question/problem "Solve $\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x-1)=\\operatorname{log}⁡(6x+9)$ for $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the logarithms on both sides of the equation have the same base, we can equate the arguments. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x-1) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(6x+9) \\\\ 2x-1 & =6x+9 \\\\ 4x & =-10 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now check for extraneous solutions by substituting back into the original equation: \n\n- Substituting $x=-\\frac{5}{2}$ back into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{log}⁡(2(-\\frac{5}{2})-1) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(6(-\\frac{5}{2})+9) \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(-6) & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{log}⁡(-6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\operatorname{log}⁡(-6)$ is not a real number.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the equation has no real solutions."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538952 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1855bada-2725-59d8-a436-9eabac0d269a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(\\frac{x-3}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538287} {:answer/id #uuid "a36db184-0ba8-51a6-a6e1-f3d0031f8eaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(\\frac{x-3}{\\sqrt{2}})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538620} {:answer/id #uuid "a53549e9-4d6a-5b8a-9f0f-45fd33da51c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(\\frac{x-3}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538625} {:answer/id #uuid "cd63ed22-de30-53b6-8fe7-0ebc4520cbe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(\\frac{x-3}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538708} {:answer/id #uuid "d187b48a-c1be-5c6a-8137-357a4d0e0d91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(\\frac{x-3}{\\sqrt{2}})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538718}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a53549e9-4d6a-5b8a-9f0f-45fd33da51c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(\\frac{x-3}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538625} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8996f52-0a1f-58ab-9d5b-d752f553fcb0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538904}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "42211531-3fd4-52dd-adb0-73d677948d1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-97907" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{6x-{x}^{2}-5}\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6x-{x}^{2}-5 & =-({x}^{2}-6x+5) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}-{3}^{2}+5) \\\\  & =-((x-3)^{2}-4) \\\\  & =4-(x-3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the denominator of our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{6x-{x}^{2}-5}\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{4-(x-3)^{2}}\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{1}{2}(x-3))}^{2}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=\\frac{1}{2}(x-3).$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=2\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{1}{2}(x-3))}^{2}}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{arcoth}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}(x-3))+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(\\frac{x-3}{2})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this result is subject to the condition $|\\frac{x-3}{2}|>1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538953 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d7c4290-9353-557a-a78e-c9968ff65543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(\\frac{3x-2}{3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538326} {:answer/id #uuid "5ce2b7d3-6d59-59f5-8ebe-05b2e387253e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(3x-2)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538433} {:answer/id #uuid "88bb5d4b-0ce6-53d9-8497-039fb7fe2e2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{18}\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(\\frac{3x-2}{3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538551} {:answer/id #uuid "c0f32036-6948-5f43-a566-222721d70200" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{18}\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(\\frac{3x-2}{3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538681} {:answer/id #uuid "d37f6b69-2bf6-5e33-a159-45bc4b373ce2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{18}\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(3x-2)+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538722}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0f32036-6948-5f43-a566-222721d70200" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{18}\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(\\frac{3x-2}{3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538681} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe2698ca-7e20-5c2e-95df-9f69c77c58a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "46c80522-dca7-526c-a225-6cc3d9755240" :question/math-academy-id "q-97313" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{(6x-4)\\sqrt{9-(3x-2)^{2}}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{(6x-4)\\sqrt{9-(3x-2)^{2}}}=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{6(x-\\frac{2}{3})\\sqrt{9-9{(x-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}}}=\\frac{1}{18}\\int \\frac{1}{(x-\\frac{2}{3})\\sqrt{1-{(x-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=x-\\frac{2}{3}.$ Then, we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{18}\\int \\frac{1}{(x-\\frac{2}{3})\\sqrt{1-{(x-\\frac{2}{3})}^{2}}}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{18}\\int \\frac{1}{u\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{18}\\operatorname{arsech}⁡u+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{18}\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(x-\\frac{2}{3})+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{18}\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(\\frac{3x-2}{3})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538961 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "365ac6de-5bc0-530c-8769-ab950b8fa19c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40\\,320\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{8})}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538344} {:answer/id #uuid "458c751d-9727-57bd-b649-b6e3f795e9f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40\\,320\\cdot {(\\frac{9}{8})}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538373} {:answer/id #uuid "826be51e-5684-5c67-9bdc-d422871db60d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40\\,320\\cdot {(\\frac{7}{8})}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538531} {:answer/id #uuid "d64d65fc-c9e4-5437-a07d-855621ae3d4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40\\,{320}^{4}\\cdot (\\frac{7}{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538726} {:answer/id #uuid "f33312dc-e9ad-50f7-8e70-313a65c252c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40\\,{320}^{4}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538781}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "826be51e-5684-5c67-9bdc-d422871db60d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40\\,320\\cdot {(\\frac{7}{8})}^{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538531} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b2bdf66-4c59-5fc0-809b-1e1cdae74143" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5e4151ac-1192-5c41-bdd3-5c80d61ad30d" :question/math-academy-id "q-103124" :question/problem "A company initially had $40\\,320$ followers on a social media platform. If the number of followers declines by $\\frac{1}{8}$ every month after some bad publicity, which expression gives approximately the number of followers after $4$ months?" :question/worked-solution "If the number of followers reduces by $\\frac{1}{8}$ every month, then $\\frac{7}{8}$ of the followers remains every month.\n\nTherefore:\n\n- After $1$ month, there will be $40\\,320\\cdot (\\frac{7}{8})$ followers.\n\n- After $2$ months, there will be $40\\,320\\cdot {(\\frac{7}{8})}^{2}$ followers.\n\n- After $3$ months, there will be approximately $40\\,320\\cdot {(\\frac{7}{8})}^{3}$ followers.\n\n- After $4$ months, there will be approximately $40\\,320\\cdot {(\\frac{7}{8})}^{4}$ followers."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538970 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19c51b78-104c-5cc3-aee1-4e68441c9623" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(\\frac{x+3}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538292} {:answer/id #uuid "453056a6-3a29-558e-96a3-8e72fe77debe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(\\frac{x+3}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538372} {:answer/id #uuid "82eda7e3-a9bb-5caf-8d13-aa3068ab29d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(\\frac{x+3}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538532} {:answer/id #uuid "a5174d35-1a1b-5a59-a41a-cfbded8edbe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(\\frac{x+3}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538624} {:answer/id #uuid "d47c671f-daea-5c22-a4cd-9f6d91df90f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(\\frac{x+3}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538724}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "453056a6-3a29-558e-96a3-8e72fe77debe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(\\frac{x+3}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538372} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e1629ae-ab1a-568d-929f-d60dc925bde5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538828}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "69f3a20c-d1a0-5eb2-90e0-cb5356408eda" :question/math-academy-id "q-97901" :question/problem "$\\int -\\frac{6}{|x+3|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+6x+13}}\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+6x+13 & ={x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}-{3}^{2}+13 \\\\  & =(x+3)^{2}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -\\frac{6}{|x+3|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+6x+13}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int \\frac{6}{|x+3|\\sqrt{4+(x+3)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{6}{2|\\frac{x+3}{2}|\\sqrt{4+4{(\\frac{x+3}{2})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{|\\frac{x+3}{2}|\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{x+3}{2})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $u=\\frac{x+3}{2}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=2\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{|\\frac{x+3}{2}|\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{x+3}{2})}^{2}}}\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{1+{u}^{2}}}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-3\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{1+{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡u+C \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(\\frac{x+3}{2})+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538972 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7aacd782-dbd4-58d3-8bc5-764458467ecc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7aacd782-dbd4-58d3-8bc5-764458467ecc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cos\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538513} :answer-field/id #uuid "e15e41c7-21f5-50db-bcda-4b29a0f68ef3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6b7d68eb-3099-57db-9579-cc9c26274e37" :question/math-academy-id "q-350681" :question/problem "What is the sum of the series $\\,1-\\frac{1}{9\\cdot 2!}+\\frac{1}{81\\cdot 4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{3}^{2n}\\cdot (2n)!}+⋯\\,?$\n\nThe sum of the series is\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We recognize that the series looks similar to the Maclaurin expansion for $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,{x}^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ into our Maclaurin expansion gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos\\,(\\frac{1}{3}) & =1-\\frac{{(1/3)}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{(1/3)}^{4}}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,{(1/3)}^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯ \\\\ cos\\,(\\frac{1}{3}) & =1-\\frac{1}{{3}^{2}\\cdot 2!}+\\frac{1}{{3}^{4}\\cdot 4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{3}^{2n}\\cdot (2n)!}+⋯ \\\\ cos\\,(\\frac{1}{3}) & =1-\\frac{1}{9\\cdot 2!}+\\frac{1}{81\\cdot 4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{3}^{2n}\\cdot (2n)!}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the sum of the series is $cos\\,(\\frac{1}{3}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538976 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3225ce6-163f-5ab0-848d-eac7a030fd95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538780}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3225ce6-163f-5ab0-848d-eac7a030fd95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538780} :answer-field/id #uuid "380c73ef-343d-548e-9c09-eeac68deca83" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538833}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "75da158e-460b-5ea3-876d-2ea3f7a43576" :question/math-academy-id "q-346635" :question/problem "![](images/47/479e2f20fea6b5e22b11fa8f8e5015ad81a57a10ed43a53d5669b0363d33b185.png)\n\nA conical grain container has a height of $450\\,\\text{cm}$ and the radius at the bottom of the container is $90\\,\\text{cm}.$ The container is being emptied so that the distance $h$ from the top of the container to the grain surface increases at a rate of $\\frac{25}{3h}\\,\\text{cm/min}.$ Find the rate at which the volume of the grain is decreasing when the grain level is $180\\,\\text{cm}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe volume of grain is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{min}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the dimensions of the container. Let's call $H=450\\,\\text{cm}$ the height of the conical container, and $R=90\\,\\text{cm}$ its radius at the bottom. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the grain level. We call $h$ the distance from the top of the container to the surface of the grain, $r$ the radius of the cone at the grain surface, and $v$ the volume of the empty space in the container. All of these quantities change with time. In particular, we are given\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{25}{3h}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min},\n$$\n\nwhich is positive because the distance from the top of the container to the grain surface increases as the level of the grain in the container decreases. \n\nWe want to know $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ when the level of the grain is $180\\,\\text{cm}.$ At this moment, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h & =H-180 \\\\  & =450-180 \\\\  & =270\\,\\text{cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the container and the empty space located between the container's top and the grain surface must be similar. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{90}{450} \\\\ r & =\\frac{h}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{h}{5})}^{2}h \\\\ v & =\\frac{1}{75}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{75}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{25}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{25}\\pi {h}^{2}(\\frac{25}{3h}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi h\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi (270) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =90\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of grain is decreasing at a rate of $90\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{min}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538980 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f21857b-1659-5d21-9156-55464bc5827c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(x+\\frac{1}{3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538401} {:answer/id #uuid "5cbbfe99-40b4-5a39-acb5-22411fdbd5b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(x+\\frac{1}{3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538432} {:answer/id #uuid "7b11f975-7008-532f-86a6-34c7ddbed852" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(x+\\frac{1}{3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538514} {:answer/id #uuid "ebe3d04d-8a52-5a06-8112-4f61eaf69eab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{9}\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(x+\\frac{1}{3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538771} {:answer/id #uuid "fb8e96b0-33c1-525d-a438-9b2176a1d06e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(x+\\frac{1}{3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538799}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb8e96b0-33c1-525d-a438-9b2176a1d06e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(x+\\frac{1}{3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538799} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e035c3a-1daf-5a2e-a5be-43c121a2a65e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "87c6c320-8ad0-5a7a-8ea7-78e763a7875c" :question/math-academy-id "q-97781" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{3}{|3x+1|\\sqrt{9{x}^{2}+6x+10}}\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}+6x+10 & =9({x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{10}{9}) \\\\  & =9({x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}x+{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+\\frac{10}{9}) \\\\  & =9[{(x+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+1]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{|3x+1|\\sqrt{9{x}^{2}+6x+10}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{|x+\\frac{1}{3}|\\sqrt{9[1+{(x+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}]}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{|x+\\frac{1}{3}|\\sqrt{1+{(x+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $u=x+\\frac{1}{3}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{|x+\\frac{1}{3}|\\sqrt{1+{(x+\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{1+{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡u+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(x+\\frac{1}{3})+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538981 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98179f33-36bb-5786-aba0-2e8a4ef467fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{25}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538592}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98179f33-36bb-5786-aba0-2e8a4ef467fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{25}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538592} :answer-field/id #uuid "377f3da4-4d13-5ed9-a0c5-00b4eaa5cff5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538832}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8ad57966-24c0-50f4-b632-7291950e6a6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-353118" :question/problem "Find the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of the geometric series \n\n$$\n-10-2-\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{2}{25}-⋯.\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n${S}_{\\infty }=$  \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFor the given geometric series, the first term ${a}_{1}=-10$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{(-2)}{(-10)}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{-10}{(1-\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{10}{(\\frac{4}{5})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{25}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538982 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bbc5aca-b66b-5326-80f6-72047f13c7d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(\\frac{x-2}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538389} {:answer/id #uuid "4fd91ee3-171f-5ecd-a04d-ece67ed21978" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(\\frac{x-2}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538403} {:answer/id #uuid "50e631f8-7a68-5ed6-948d-c6cd8d569be1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(\\frac{x-2}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538406} {:answer/id #uuid "b6d77791-2d19-57bb-83b2-1fc061951977" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(\\frac{x-2}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538657} {:answer/id #uuid "babf514f-5d4d-5f68-a29b-d4e542505f1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(\\frac{x-2}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "50e631f8-7a68-5ed6-948d-c6cd8d569be1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(\\frac{x-2}{4})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538406} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ba0e725-7b1f-5872-b603-123fc8faa2d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8dc0f52e-b98b-54ec-bcf4-1fa4702113db" :question/math-academy-id "q-98005" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{12+4x-{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12+4x-{x}^{2} & =-({x}^{2}-4x-12) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2}-{2}^{2}-12) \\\\  & =-((x-2)^{2}-16) \\\\  & =16-(x-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the denominator of our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{12+4x-{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{16-(x-2)^{2}}\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{16}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{1}{4}(x-2))}^{2}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=\\frac{1}{4}(x-2).$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{4}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=4\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{16}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{1}{4}(x-2))}^{2}}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{16}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\cdot 4\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{arcoth}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(\\frac{1}{4}(x-2))+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(\\frac{x-2}{4})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this result is subject to the condition $|\\frac{x-2}{4}|>1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538986 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "058b950b-7211-5b63-a8ef-ca5da43d6e22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538246} {:answer/id #uuid "3286958d-7c74-5b1d-87b0-93f4fb2b3417" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538336} {:answer/id #uuid "8022af58-b7b6-5f7d-b6df-8a0168170bbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538525} {:answer/id #uuid "b8c58dde-7497-55fd-9509-60437b98844f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538665} {:answer/id #uuid "d6ca12e2-aa98-5c4a-ba2d-ac826140d46c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538728}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8022af58-b7b6-5f7d-b6df-8a0168170bbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538525} :answer-field/id #uuid "ccbfad55-03ca-511b-aff7-effb313d79ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9b70f694-75c4-56a5-8060-3f93ae37aa40" :question/math-academy-id "q-103098" :question/problem "What is the sum of the series $\\frac{5\\pi }{6}-\\frac{(5\\pi /6)^{3}}{3!}+\\frac{(5\\pi /6)^{5}}{5!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}(5\\pi /6)^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!}+⋯\\,?$" :question/worked-solution "We recognize that the series looks similar to the Maclaurin expansion for $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}+\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}{x}^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ into our Maclaurin expansion gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}-\\frac{(5\\pi /6)^{3}}{3!}+\\frac{(5\\pi /6)^{5}}{5!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}(5\\pi /6)^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!}+⋯ \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}-\\frac{(5\\pi /6)^{3}}{3!}+\\frac{(5\\pi /6)^{5}}{5!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}(5\\pi /6)^{2n+1}}{(2n+1)!}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the series is $\\frac{1}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538989 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3de78098-2f30-5690-a297-c93784039e6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538358} {:answer/id #uuid "5fb7824d-c0c6-5aa8-963a-415d8fba6461" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538443} {:answer/id #uuid "b514ce4c-c0a9-56cf-b77b-d76239980c0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538653} {:answer/id #uuid "dce2f00a-8e3b-5f21-85eb-ac015d859b8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538739} {:answer/id #uuid "ebb12994-1f15-5905-ac9f-a9912f0659fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.25\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538770}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b514ce4c-c0a9-56cf-b77b-d76239980c0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538653} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3b0ce1e-2140-5aca-87cb-414958166a8a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538891}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a327d128-352b-50f9-99e7-ff67c5f44d4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-31996" :question/problem "![](images/13/135ce8e277c0895d36972e12fb7df68447af79765c4acaaba6fb0e63fc498d73.png)\n\nThe height of an ice-cream cone is $6\\,\\text{cm}$ and the radius at the top of the cone is $1.5\\,\\text{cm}.$ Some ice-cream is leaking from a small hole at the bottom at a rate of $\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{min}.$ If the ice-cream level is $4\\,\\text{cm}$ at a particular moment, how fast is the ice-cream level decreasing at that moment?" :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the dimensions of the cone. Let's call $H=6\\,\\text{cm}$ the height of the cone, and $R=1.5\\,\\text{cm}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the ice cream level. We call $h$ the height of the ice cream in the cone and $r$ the radius of the ice cream top surface. Both of these quantities, and the ice cream volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{min},\n$$\n\nand we want to find the rate of change of the ice cream level $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}$ when $h=4\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of the ice cream:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cone and the ice cream part of the cone must be similar. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{1.5}{6} \\\\ r & =\\frac{h}{4}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{h}{4})}^{2}h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{48}\\pi {h}^{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{48}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{16}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the rate of change of the ice cream level in the cone when $h=4\\,\\text{cm}.$ We can compute the value of $h$ at this moment as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\pi  & =\\frac{1}{16}\\pi ({4}^{2})\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-1\\,\\text{cm/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the height of the ice cream is decreasing at a rate of $1\\,\\text{cm/min}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538998 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "277698a4-b642-5828-bd44-26f327d9872a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538311} {:answer/id #uuid "786f03b1-91d3-5f0b-98d1-31ea6b606b45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538504} {:answer/id #uuid "7f8df014-f766-508e-ae02-8b1cf949fa8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{1}{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538523} {:answer/id #uuid "a132b4e2-4cf1-5445-83d6-b3de269c66b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538616} {:answer/id #uuid "de32adf5-d49b-5e95-96c0-dc0ee2551230" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538741}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "277698a4-b642-5828-bd44-26f327d9872a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538311} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf45e113-afc9-589f-ba9f-87e14316c2aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538901}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b2664ee6-8acb-52ad-889d-ed63a283e637" :question/math-academy-id "q-103117" :question/problem "What is the sum of the series $\\,1-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{64\\cdot 2!}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{4}}{4\\,096\\cdot 4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,{\\pi }^{2n}}{{8}^{2n}\\cdot (2n)!}+⋯\\,?$" :question/worked-solution "We recognize that the series looks similar to the Maclaurin expansion for $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4!}-⋯-\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,{x}^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ into our Maclaurin expansion gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =1-\\frac{{(\\pi /8)}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{(\\pi /8)}^{4}}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,{(\\pi /8)}^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯ \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =1-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{{8}^{2}\\cdot 2!}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{4}}{{8}^{4}\\cdot 4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,{\\pi }^{2n}}{{8}^{2n}\\cdot (2n)!}+⋯ \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}) & =1-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{64\\cdot 2!}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{4}}{4\\,096\\cdot 4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,{\\pi }^{2n}}{{8}^{2n}\\cdot (2n)!}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the sum of the series is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539002 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dbfd70f-5e32-5f4c-9459-b0ae4b834535" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538297} {:answer/id #uuid "475c219c-d8f4-5ecb-aef0-762429b48a5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538381} {:answer/id #uuid "8d9d7b6b-6fdb-5ed5-bcb4-eab5156b4447" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538563} {:answer/id #uuid "b8085e5d-331f-508c-9e6f-d87756cc2771" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538662} {:answer/id #uuid "f79f2a31-fef1-5434-90a8-26e88176d330" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538795}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d9d7b6b-6fdb-5ed5-bcb4-eab5156b4447" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538563} :answer-field/id #uuid "25570059-2136-5352-9294-04bc3533b43e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538819}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b939d5df-d459-5fd4-9292-d3dcc1c6afac" :question/math-academy-id "q-50942" :question/problem "$\\,1-\\frac{(\\pi /2)^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{(\\pi /2)^{4}}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,{(\\pi /2)}^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯=$" :question/worked-solution "We recognize that the series looks similar to the Maclaurin expansion for $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,{x}^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into our Maclaurin expansion gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =1-\\frac{{(\\pi /2)}^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{{(\\pi /2)}^{4}}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,{(\\pi /2)}^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯ \\\\ 0 & =1-\\frac{(\\pi /2)^{2}}{2!}+\\frac{(\\pi /2)^{4}}{4!}-⋯+\\frac{(-1)^{n}\\,{(\\pi /2)}^{2n}}{(2n)!}+⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the sum of the series is $0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539005 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46ee4abf-f8a1-5118-9471-a54db96f45a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{7}\\times {(\\frac{1}{5})}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538378} {:answer/id #uuid "69e93b7b-b1d1-5cb1-973f-7ccaf1e39a29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{4}^{5}}{7\\times 5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538469} {:answer/id #uuid "cb92c9ab-11a4-5e5d-bb15-e3eb6dedabbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{4}{7})}^{5}\\times \\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538706} {:answer/id #uuid "d0058a23-5040-59e0-a92d-9b29b1549ea3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{4}{7})}^{5}\\times {(\\frac{1}{5})}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538715} {:answer/id #uuid "d6feceb1-2fa4-5151-ba55-921a2c546541" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{{7}^{5}\\times {5}^{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538729}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0058a23-5040-59e0-a92d-9b29b1549ea3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(\\frac{4}{7})}^{5}\\times {(\\frac{1}{5})}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538715} :answer-field/id #uuid "544faafb-45af-51f7-9886-1c44cb2fbaea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c32986e7-9010-5588-b701-72ad7388c062" :question/math-academy-id "q-68615" :question/problem "${(\\frac{4}{7}\\times \\frac{1}{5})}^{5}=$" :question/worked-solution "To raise the product of two numbers to the power of $5$, we can raise each number to the power of $5,$ and then multiply:\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{4}{7}\\times \\frac{1}{5})}^{4}={(\\frac{4}{7})}^{5}\\times {(\\frac{1}{5})}^{5}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539016 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e2a94b3-44dd-52b7-9ec8-57361cc9534d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(\\frac{x+3}{\\sqrt{2}})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538267} {:answer/id #uuid "5ede2391-c2b2-5c77-84a5-0c2d651d0c65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(\\frac{x+3}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538441} {:answer/id #uuid "7ecac083-f3f6-50fd-85bd-f274abf8632b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(\\frac{x+3}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538522} {:answer/id #uuid "8f9a2895-a913-526c-a6b5-9b164f01bfb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(\\frac{x+3}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538569} {:answer/id #uuid "c0b5fe7e-e088-5647-ad7c-cb6d53c10815" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(\\frac{x+3}{\\sqrt{2}})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538680}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ede2391-c2b2-5c77-84a5-0c2d651d0c65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(\\frac{x+3}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538441} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f32ba7e-ec4d-5979-a816-0eff8d932fa9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dbcc5371-79bd-5123-b706-5a10821a5d21" :question/math-academy-id "q-97758" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{4-(x+3)^{2}}\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{4-(x+3)^{2}}\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{4-4{(\\frac{x+3}{2})}^{2}}\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{x+3}{2})}^{2}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=\\frac{x+3}{2}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=2\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{x+3}{2})}^{2}}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{arcoth}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(\\frac{x+3}{2})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this result is subject to the condition $|\\frac{x+3}{2}|>1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539017 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "477b4b85-509c-50c5-94e2-30b9c718e7a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(\\frac{x-6}{3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538382} {:answer/id #uuid "6cb6bcbe-491f-5c80-b732-790eda522c86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(\\frac{x-6}{3})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538476} {:answer/id #uuid "97f3f503-2f88-57dd-99bb-2fd2911f3dea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(\\frac{x-6}{\\sqrt{3}})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538591} {:answer/id #uuid "a267b645-2e3e-5684-b73a-ee1553ef4a64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(\\frac{x-6}{\\sqrt{3}})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538618} {:answer/id #uuid "d701ac5e-f7fc-59a7-8172-9671e49711d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(\\frac{x-6}{\\sqrt{3}})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538730}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d701ac5e-f7fc-59a7-8172-9671e49711d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(\\frac{x-6}{\\sqrt{3}})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538730} :answer-field/id #uuid "0edb72e4-359e-521e-a337-ecd9f317a1a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538810}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dc07885c-59ca-5761-a1b0-43a8e8cb6e2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-98008" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{12x-{x}^{2}-33}\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12x-{x}^{2}-33 & =-({x}^{2}-12x+33) \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 6x+{6}^{2}-{6}^{2}+33) \\\\  & =-((x-6)^{2}-3) \\\\  & =3-(x-6)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the denominator of our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{12x-{x}^{2}-33}\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{3-(x-6)^{2}}\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}(x-6))}^{2}}\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $u=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}(x-6).$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}(x-6))}^{2}}\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\int \\frac{1}{1-{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{arcoth}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\cdot \\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}(x-6))+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\operatorname{arcoth}⁡(\\frac{x-6}{\\sqrt{3}})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that this result is subject to the condition $|\\frac{x-6}{\\sqrt{3}}|>1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539028 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45abf4a2-106d-55be-98fe-a203ed7f3489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(\\frac{x+1}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538374} {:answer/id #uuid "650fbbcf-b967-5e75-a0a8-d383d4231cca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(\\frac{x+1}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538457} {:answer/id #uuid "8a07f14a-602d-5dd1-aeec-f618cd8ac959" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(\\frac{x+1}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538554} {:answer/id #uuid "b64538dd-a81f-5c56-9eaa-8ba0ac2c899d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arsech}⁡(\\frac{x+1}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538656} {:answer/id #uuid "dabd251e-fae7-5698-83c0-507011d42bdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(\\frac{x+1}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538734}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dabd251e-fae7-5698-83c0-507011d42bdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(\\frac{x+1}{2})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538734} :answer-field/id #uuid "59703bd9-6018-583d-92ce-e008586da72f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538852}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fa581feb-4c4c-5d1e-ad5b-763e0315938f" :question/math-academy-id "q-97780" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{4}{|x+1|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2x+5}}\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we complete the square for the argument of the square root in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2x+5 & ={x}^{2}+2\\cdot x+{1}^{2}-{1}^{2}+5 \\\\  & =(x+1)^{2}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the given integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4}{|x+1|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+2x+5}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{4}{|x+1|\\sqrt{4+(x+1)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2}{|\\frac{x+1}{2}|\\sqrt{4+4{(\\frac{x+1}{2})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{|\\frac{x+1}{2}|\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{x+1}{2})}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $u=\\frac{x+1}{2}.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=2\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{|\\frac{x+1}{2}|\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{x+1}{2})}^{2}}}\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{1+{u}^{2}}}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{1+{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡u+C \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{arcsch}⁡(\\frac{x+1}{2})+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539122 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f1da427-cfa7-5281-8204-d21535a2e929" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\frac{3}{2}}\\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 13} \\left(6 t^{3} - 24 t^{2} - 18 t - 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539136} {:answer/id #uuid "35771a76-e706-5927-92ab-462e6d1f85c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\frac{3}{2}}6 t^{3} - 24 t^{2} - 18 t - 3\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539138} {:answer/id #uuid "3f2e2937-ae52-5040-b66a-a5c64838d0de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\frac{3}{2}}- 3 \\left(4 t^{2} + 13\\right) \\left(- 2 t^{3} + 8 t^{2} + 6 t + 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539142} {:answer/id #uuid "4a9e8407-ee17-513a-9905-7a045102dae7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\frac{3}{2}}3 \\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 13} \\left(t^{2} \\left(2 t + 1\\right) + \\left(3 t + 1\\right)^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539147} {:answer/id #uuid "8353a5ce-5ec8-58d9-82a4-deeedafa3521" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\frac{3}{2}}\\frac{3 \\left(2 t^{3} - 8 t^{2} - 6 t - 1\\right)}{\\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 13}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539169}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f1da427-cfa7-5281-8204-d21535a2e929" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\frac{3}{2}}\\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 13} \\left(6 t^{3} - 24 t^{2} - 18 t - 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539136} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d63b3a0-c096-5219-817e-9b3bad04d2cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "021666da-2b39-563b-b7fb-5efa8b86fda8" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\int_C 3 x y - 3 z^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=\\langle t^{2}, 2 t + 1, 3 t + 1\\rangle$ for $\\frac{1}{2}\\le t\\le \\frac{3}{2}$?" :question/worked-solution "Since $r(t)=\\langle t^{2}, 2 t + 1, 3 t + 1\\rangle,$ then along the curve we have $x=t^{2}$, $y=2 t + 1$, $z=3 t + 1$.\n\nComputing $r'(t)$ and $\\|r'(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,k \\\\ &= \\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t^{2})i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2 t + 1)j+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(3 t + 1)k \\\\ &= (2 t)\\,i+(2)\\,j+(3)\\,k \\\\ \\|r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2+(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2+(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{(2 t)^2+(2)^2+(3)^2} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=3 x y - 3 z^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=3\\left((t^{2})(2 t + 1)-(3 t + 1)^2\\right) \\\\ &= 3\\left(2t^3+(1)t^2-\\left(9t^2+(6)t+1\\right)\\right) \\\\ &= 6 t^{3} - 24 t^{2} - 18 t - 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\frac{3}{2}} f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= \\int_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\frac{3}{2}}(6 t^{3} - 24 t^{2} - 18 t - 3)(\\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 13})\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= \\int_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\frac{3}{2}}\\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 13} \\left(6 t^{3} - 24 t^{2} - 18 t - 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539128 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18bbf45e-712a-50fc-9c7f-3db24fc3f7da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{\\frac{3}{2}}\\frac{2 \\left(- t^{3} + 7 t^{2} + 4 t + 1\\right)}{\\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 5}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539127} {:answer/id #uuid "4b8011db-7762-523b-8d12-4da85216dfd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{\\frac{3}{2}}- 2 t^{3} + 14 t^{2} + 8 t + 2\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539149} {:answer/id #uuid "a46660c1-17ae-5ef0-8a37-70323fa5fabb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{\\frac{3}{2}}2 \\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 5} \\left(- t^{3} - t^{2} - 4 t - 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539175} {:answer/id #uuid "ad8ff517-6e7d-547d-aa23-f428cf620502" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{\\frac{3}{2}}2 \\left(4 t^{2} + 5\\right) \\left(- t^{3} + 7 t^{2} + 4 t + 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539179} {:answer/id #uuid "dfc8dcdb-9950-52f1-b6f2-4fb28f2f94be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{\\frac{3}{2}}2 \\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 5} \\left(- t^{3} + 7 t^{2} + 4 t + 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539193}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dfc8dcdb-9950-52f1-b6f2-4fb28f2f94be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{\\frac{3}{2}}2 \\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 5} \\left(- t^{3} + 7 t^{2} + 4 t + 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539193} :answer-field/id #uuid "69673806-2d1a-558a-87aa-962b7675aa65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539159}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "19471cdf-9ea5-5c33-ad36-e0767c3ac883" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\int_C - 2 x y + 2 z^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=\\langle t^{2}, t - 3, 2 t + 1\\rangle$ for $1\\le t\\le \\frac{3}{2}$?" :question/worked-solution "Since $r(t)=\\langle t^{2}, t - 3, 2 t + 1\\rangle,$ then along the curve we have $x=t^{2}$, $y=t - 3$, $z=2 t + 1$.\n\nComputing $r'(t)$ and $\\|r'(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,k \\\\ &= \\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t^{2})i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t - 3)j+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2 t + 1)k \\\\ &= (2 t)\\,i+(1)\\,j+(2)\\,k \\\\ \\|r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2+(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2+(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{(2 t)^2+(1)^2+(2)^2} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=- 2 x y + 2 z^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=-2\\left((t^{2})(t - 3)-(2 t + 1)^2\\right) \\\\ &= -2\\left(1t^3+(-3)t^2-\\left(4t^2+(4)t+1\\right)\\right) \\\\ &= - 2 t^{3} + 14 t^{2} + 8 t + 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{1}^{\\frac{3}{2}} f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= \\int_{1}^{\\frac{3}{2}}(- 2 t^{3} + 14 t^{2} + 8 t + 2)(\\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 5})\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= \\int_{1}^{\\frac{3}{2}}2 \\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 5} \\left(- t^{3} + 7 t^{2} + 4 t + 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539129 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36c637e7-9d1d-5a19-bf0e-0dfdcc062824" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{2}\\frac{4 t^{3} - 5 t^{2} - 6 t - 1}{\\sqrt{16 t^{2} + 13}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539139} {:answer/id #uuid "5aa6780b-c1c7-5d0f-bc04-322cd3efb4a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{2}4 t^{3} - 5 t^{2} - 6 t - 1\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539152} {:answer/id #uuid "805163b1-91d9-5657-84ba-f0adcd279e39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{2}- \\left(16 t^{2} + 13\\right) \\left(- 4 t^{3} + 5 t^{2} + 6 t + 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539167} {:answer/id #uuid "c792bb24-aaaf-56f9-a31e-683b56a1218d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{16 t^{2} + 13} \\left(4 t^{2} \\left(t + 1\\right) + \\left(3 t + 1\\right)^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539186} {:answer/id #uuid "d82355ef-7111-535b-bc6f-528e24822529" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{16 t^{2} + 13} \\left(4 t^{3} - 5 t^{2} - 6 t - 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539191}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d82355ef-7111-535b-bc6f-528e24822529" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{16 t^{2} + 13} \\left(4 t^{3} - 5 t^{2} - 6 t - 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539191} :answer-field/id #uuid "507606cb-45d1-5506-805d-8a68afcce084" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539150}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1b6b1bd1-88e4-5d2e-9dfb-33372f16d858" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\int_C x y - z^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=\\langle 2 t^{2}, 2 t + 2, 3 t + 1\\rangle$ for $0\\le t\\le 2$?" :question/worked-solution "Since $r(t)=\\langle 2 t^{2}, 2 t + 2, 3 t + 1\\rangle,$ then along the curve we have $x=2 t^{2}$, $y=2 t + 2$, $z=3 t + 1$.\n\nComputing $r'(t)$ and $\\|r'(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,k \\\\ &= \\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2 t^{2})i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2 t + 2)j+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(3 t + 1)k \\\\ &= (4 t)\\,i+(2)\\,j+(3)\\,k \\\\ \\|r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2+(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2+(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{(4 t)^2+(2)^2+(3)^2} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{16 t^{2} + 13}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=x y - z^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=(2 t^{2})(2 t + 2)-(3 t + 1)^2 \\\\ &= 4t^3+(4)t^2-\\left(9t^2+(6)t+1\\right) \\\\ &= 4 t^{3} - 5 t^{2} - 6 t - 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{0}^{2} f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= \\int_{0}^{2}(4 t^{3} - 5 t^{2} - 6 t - 1)(\\sqrt{16 t^{2} + 13})\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= \\int_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{16 t^{2} + 13} \\left(4 t^{3} - 5 t^{2} - 6 t - 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539157 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a182349-c6b0-5892-a095-c61253000a65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{5}\\frac{2 t}{4 t^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539151} {:answer/id #uuid "808a5bee-fd16-5441-99c5-68b38ff67c63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{5}2 t\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539168} {:answer/id #uuid "9950a772-8737-542f-989a-2f5006693b2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{5}\\frac{2 t}{\\left(4 t^{2} + 1\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539173} {:answer/id #uuid "a5a1d7be-903b-525d-a61b-b6fd648c3cb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{5}\\frac{2 t^{2}}{\\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539177} {:answer/id #uuid "e8983d55-5bea-5b37-b969-63bf075c738a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{5}\\frac{2 t}{\\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539199}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8983d55-5bea-5b37-b969-63bf075c738a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{5}\\frac{2 t}{\\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539199} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7d2f535-cd02-5984-8b38-b25fd712257c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539198}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "629bb41a-481a-5fce-89d8-1176b4e371da" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}2\\left(\\frac{x}{4y+1}\\right)\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j\\,$ for $0\\le t\\le 5?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j,$ then along the curve we have $x=t$ and $y={t}^{2}.$ \n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})j \\\\  & =i+2tj \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=2\\left(\\frac{x}{4y+1}\\right)$, then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2\\left(\\frac{t}{4{t}^{2}+1}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}2\\left(\\frac{x}{4y+1}\\right)\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t  \\\\    & =2\\left({\\int }_{0}^{5}(\\frac{t}{4{t}^{2}+1})\\cdot \\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\cdot \\text{d}t\\right) \\\\    & =2\\left({\\int }_{0}^{5}\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}}\\text{d}t\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539161 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "278b3e92-0930-54df-8b78-9d1eb2eb134a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{12}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\sqrt{2} t\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539133} {:answer/id #uuid "27d15098-9f4d-5025-b663-55253273ac10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{12}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\frac{\\sqrt{2} t \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539134} {:answer/id #uuid "e3e304d5-2e98-55e6-88b1-e67c5a9ca1c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{12}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}t \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539194} {:answer/id #uuid "e45f8e18-3e75-5cb3-8aee-472302b82414" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{12}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\sqrt{2} t \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539195} {:answer/id #uuid "fe1b2043-64b1-5186-9ca4-d5be02457609" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{12}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}2 t \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e45f8e18-3e75-5cb3-8aee-472302b82414" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{12}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\sqrt{2} t \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539195} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1b51734-7cf3-59d4-b15c-c37a8d472ced" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539189}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "77c0932d-edde-5a3d-a95b-c9e88ef0198f" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j\\,+t\\,k$ for $\\,\\frac{\\pi}{12}\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j\\,+t\\,k,$ then along the curve we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ and $z=t.$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow, if $f(x,y,z)=z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =t((\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}) \\\\  & =t({cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{12}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{12}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{12}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539171 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bbf7d63-fae5-55c0-b85a-adf23a9e249f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{2}^{\\frac{11}{2}}\\frac{2 t^{2}}{\\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539130} {:answer/id #uuid "45af7fa4-3c7f-5937-a136-75ca8b278b25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{2}^{\\frac{11}{2}}2 t\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539144} {:answer/id #uuid "b21d5ca9-b166-5274-a50b-458ef7c02100" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{2}^{\\frac{11}{2}}\\frac{2 t}{\\left(4 t^{2} + 1\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539181} {:answer/id #uuid "c9985de4-28ed-5456-ad68-a38e965bae9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{2}^{\\frac{11}{2}}\\frac{2 t}{\\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539187} {:answer/id #uuid "e55bbb40-be6b-5a87-aca7-130550b420f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{2}^{\\frac{11}{2}}\\frac{2 t}{4 t^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539197}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9985de4-28ed-5456-ad68-a38e965bae9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{2}^{\\frac{11}{2}}\\frac{2 t}{\\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539187} :answer-field/id #uuid "20ce0eb2-a951-5877-b687-deea0d30c7a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539132}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "86c6a686-849e-56ac-8cd5-66fc10eebbef" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}2\\left(\\frac{x}{4y+1}\\right)\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j\\,$ for $2\\le t\\le \\frac{11}{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j,$ then along the curve we have $x=t$ and $y={t}^{2}.$ \n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})j \\\\  & =i+2tj \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=2\\left(\\frac{x}{4y+1}\\right)$, then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2\\left(\\frac{t}{4{t}^{2}+1}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}2\\left(\\frac{x}{4y+1}\\right)\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{2}^{\\frac{11}{2}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t  \\\\    & =2\\left({\\int }_{2}^{\\frac{11}{2}}(\\frac{t}{4{t}^{2}+1})\\cdot \\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\cdot \\text{d}t\\right) \\\\    & =2\\left({\\int }_{2}^{\\frac{11}{2}}\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}}\\text{d}t\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539172 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ddac435-f4c5-5790-a848-0d7fd8aecc12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}10 t \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539131} {:answer/id #uuid "49e747ab-2e87-5b28-b3b1-52bfbfa14251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}5 \\sqrt{2} t \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539145} {:answer/id #uuid "4a119ce1-666c-589c-913e-71a12513c765" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}\\frac{5 \\sqrt{2} t \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539146} {:answer/id #uuid "7f1d6599-ceb5-5eed-83dc-ed06fd4e1d46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}5 \\sqrt{2} t\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539165} {:answer/id #uuid "804f7a7d-ca0c-5b72-99dd-1e2bd2065c4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}5 t \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49e747ab-2e87-5b28-b3b1-52bfbfa14251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}5 \\sqrt{2} t \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539145} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4985361-8dc5-544a-a26a-fb6bff95a3f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539196}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "88d1c090-6bb7-523f-a9f3-d7ec7807cd7a" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}5\\left(z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\right)\\,\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j\\,+t\\,k$ for $\\,0\\le t\\le \\frac{\\pi}{6}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j\\,+t\\,k,$ then along the curve we have $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,$ $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ and $z=t.$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNow, if $f(x,y,z)=5\\left(z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\right)$, then we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =5\\left(t((\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2})\\right) \\\\    & =5\\left(t({cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\right) \\\\    & =5\\left(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}5\\left(z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\right)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t  \\\\    & =5\\left({\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t\\right) \\\\    & =5\\left(\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,\\text{d}t\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539174 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04ec66b2-9271-5a86-bba6-39b76534dbf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\frac{7}{3}}\\frac{5 t}{4 t^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539124} {:answer/id #uuid "39eb83de-83c7-5476-b2e1-42b4fe358621" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\frac{7}{3}}5 t\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539140} {:answer/id #uuid "3a918622-26b5-5fae-b7b9-05af71744ab8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\frac{7}{3}}\\frac{5 t}{\\left(4 t^{2} + 1\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539141} {:answer/id #uuid "60aab06d-1253-59b6-b9bd-40fdc6f9eb1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\frac{7}{3}}\\frac{5 t}{\\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539156} {:answer/id #uuid "7b390292-66dd-5f10-a384-8ca7ed8cf17b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\frac{7}{3}}\\frac{5 t^{2}}{\\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539162}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60aab06d-1253-59b6-b9bd-40fdc6f9eb1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\frac{7}{3}}\\frac{5 t}{\\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539156} :answer-field/id #uuid "af41d808-87d0-52a5-979b-295e732e80de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539180}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a17cfaab-14e7-5c09-ade5-3176e170f53b" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}5\\left(\\frac{x}{4y+1}\\right)\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j\\,$ for $\\frac{1}{2}\\le t\\le \\frac{7}{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j,$ then along the curve we have $x=t$ and $y={t}^{2}.$ \n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})j \\\\  & =i+2tj \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=5\\left(\\frac{x}{4y+1}\\right)$, then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =5\\left(\\frac{t}{4{t}^{2}+1}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}5\\left(\\frac{x}{4y+1}\\right)\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\frac{7}{3}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t  \\\\    & =5\\left({\\int }_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\frac{7}{3}}(\\frac{t}{4{t}^{2}+1})\\cdot \\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\cdot \\text{d}t\\right) \\\\    & =5\\left({\\int }_{\\frac{1}{2}}^{\\frac{7}{3}}\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}}\\text{d}t\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539178 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6007b4c8-5fd7-5182-b9b1-b8ab65716cc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{5}{2}}\\sqrt{36 t^{2} + 5} \\left(- 6 t^{3} - 10 t^{2} + 16 t + 8\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539155} {:answer/id #uuid "c10d389c-0aa7-5b23-82b4-246c7ac9ce69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{5}{2}}- 2 \\left(36 t^{2} + 5\\right) \\left(3 t^{3} + 5 t^{2} - 8 t - 4\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539184} {:answer/id #uuid "d7c7ac66-9df2-5528-8428-8ab1dc9902f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{5}{2}}\\frac{2 \\left(- 3 t^{3} - 5 t^{2} + 8 t + 4\\right)}{\\sqrt{36 t^{2} + 5}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539190} {:answer/id #uuid "df5c3f33-dfe4-50f6-8249-42490daafec4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{5}{2}}- 6 t^{3} - 10 t^{2} + 16 t + 8\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539192} {:answer/id #uuid "f5a7e9b2-2d21-55ba-a040-b5b1c6d8da08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{5}{2}}2 \\sqrt{36 t^{2} + 5} \\left(- 3 t^{2} \\left(t + 3\\right) - 4 \\left(t + 1\\right)^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539203}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6007b4c8-5fd7-5182-b9b1-b8ab65716cc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{\\frac{5}{2}}\\sqrt{36 t^{2} + 5} \\left(- 6 t^{3} - 10 t^{2} + 16 t + 8\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539155} :answer-field/id #uuid "66661945-6b6a-5b27-bb7a-9979aec89f63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539158}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a908ad08-fd2d-5338-a3b2-fa3636e47ff4" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\int_C - 2 x y + 2 z^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=\\langle 3 t^{2}, t + 3, 2 t + 2\\rangle$ for $0\\le t\\le \\frac{5}{2}$?" :question/worked-solution "Since $r(t)=\\langle 3 t^{2}, t + 3, 2 t + 2\\rangle,$ then along the curve we have $x=3 t^{2}$, $y=t + 3$, $z=2 t + 2$.\n\nComputing $r'(t)$ and $\\|r'(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,k \\\\ &= \\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(3 t^{2})i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t + 3)j+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2 t + 2)k \\\\ &= (6 t)\\,i+(1)\\,j+(2)\\,k \\\\ \\|r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2+(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2+(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{(6 t)^2+(1)^2+(2)^2} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{36 t^{2} + 5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=- 2 x y + 2 z^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=-2\\left((3 t^{2})(t + 3)-(2 t + 2)^2\\right) \\\\ &= -2\\left(3t^3+(9)t^2-\\left(4t^2+(8)t+4\\right)\\right) \\\\ &= - 6 t^{3} - 10 t^{2} + 16 t + 8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{0}^{\\frac{5}{2}} f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= \\int_{0}^{\\frac{5}{2}}(- 6 t^{3} - 10 t^{2} + 16 t + 8)(\\sqrt{36 t^{2} + 5})\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= \\int_{0}^{\\frac{5}{2}}\\sqrt{36 t^{2} + 5} \\left(- 6 t^{3} - 10 t^{2} + 16 t + 8\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539200 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27d55e55-4da0-5e7e-b523-3a71c34060d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{\\frac{4}{3}}t\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539135} {:answer/id #uuid "7b5b324f-dc6f-5f53-96c6-b0679ba8cf94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{\\frac{4}{3}}\\frac{t^{2}}{\\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539163} {:answer/id #uuid "8698b24c-571c-586d-9aa9-791ca92cca74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{\\frac{4}{3}}\\frac{t}{\\left(4 t^{2} + 1\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539170} {:answer/id #uuid "b26b07de-bd8f-5b41-9dc8-2063df8ca6ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{\\frac{4}{3}}\\frac{t}{4 t^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539182} {:answer/id #uuid "d04b5623-2bff-542f-8d52-93366f70ddc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{\\frac{4}{3}}\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539188}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d04b5623-2bff-542f-8d52-93366f70ddc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{\\frac{4}{3}}\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{4 t^{2} + 1}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539188} :answer-field/id #uuid "06daf866-2151-53ab-a697-9d6282e0ae9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539125}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f04c0a98-4a6c-5873-883f-948e18d8b72b" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}\\frac{x}{4y+1}\\text{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j\\,$ for $1\\le t\\le \\frac{4}{3}?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j,$ then along the curve we have $x=t$ and $y={t}^{2}.$ \n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})j \\\\  & =i+2tj \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, if  $f(x,y)=\\frac{x}{4y+1}$, then we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{t}{4{t}^{2}+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{x}{4y+1}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\frac{4}{3}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\frac{4}{3}}(\\frac{t}{4{t}^{2}+1})\\cdot \\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{\\frac{4}{3}}\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+1}}\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539202 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0228b294-5832-5825-ad8a-3bc0a9bdbe23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{- \\frac{3}{2}}^{2}\\frac{2 \\sqrt{5} \\left(8 t^{3} + t - 2\\right)}{5}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539123} {:answer/id #uuid "0f75487c-a5f1-5e36-86b8-989567defbd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{- \\frac{3}{2}}^{2}80 t^{3} + 10 t - 20\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539126} {:answer/id #uuid "7dea96ab-5a7e-592e-944f-a693c36c797b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{- \\frac{3}{2}}^{2}16 t^{3} + 2 t - 4\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539164} {:answer/id #uuid "c6680a8a-cce7-55d0-b883-50e0e3ba2e83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{- \\frac{3}{2}}^{2}2 \\sqrt{5} \\left(8 t^{3} - t + 2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539185} {:answer/id #uuid "f38f729c-48d5-5112-b120-4a7a2e085fed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\int_{- \\frac{3}{2}}^{2}2 \\sqrt{5} \\left(8 t^{3} + t - 2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539201}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f38f729c-48d5-5112-b120-4a7a2e085fed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\int_{- \\frac{3}{2}}^{2}2 \\sqrt{5} \\left(8 t^{3} + t - 2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t$." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539201} :answer-field/id #uuid "b97f8f35-3ac8-510f-857f-73a5203bed44" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539183}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f515b153-b2d5-5f07-b698-2b223e35049e" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_C2\\left((x^{3} - y)\\right)ds$, where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t) = 2ti + (2 - t)j$ for $- \\frac{3}{2}\\le t\\le 2$?" :question/worked-solution "Since $r(t)=\\langle 2 t, 2 - t\\rangle,$ then along the curve we have $x=2 t$, $y=2 - t$.\n\nComputing $r'(t)$ and $\\|r'(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j \\\\ &= \\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2 t)i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2 - t)j \\\\ &= (2)\\,i+(-1)\\,j \\\\ \\|r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2+(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{(2)^2+(-1)^2} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2 x^{3} - 2 y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=2\\left((2 t)^3-(2 - t)\\right) \\\\ &= 2\\left(8t^3-2+t\\right) \\\\ &= 16 t^{3} + 2 t - 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{- \\frac{3}{2}}^{2} f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= \\int_{- \\frac{3}{2}}^{2}(16 t^{3} + 2 t - 4)(\\sqrt{5})\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= \\int_{- \\frac{3}{2}}^{2}2 \\sqrt{5} \\left(8 t^{3} + t - 2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539204 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30338bca-6ca0-51af-8f2f-96eb86969268" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{\\frac{3}{2}}12 t^{3} + 3 t^{2} - 18 t - 27\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539137} {:answer/id #uuid "4a9fac12-ab2b-5bd8-ac50-2e5e3c1a9b49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{\\frac{3}{2}}3 \\sqrt{16 t^{2} + 5} \\left(4 t^{3} + t^{2} - 6 t - 9\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539148} {:answer/id #uuid "5d90c9ff-4b45-5a51-ad47-6931f43284f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{\\frac{3}{2}}3 \\sqrt{16 t^{2} + 5} \\left(2 t^{2} \\left(2 t + 1\\right) + \\left(t + 3\\right)^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539154} {:answer/id #uuid "741d6ee7-19d1-5aef-a755-d8ed6ebfa877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{\\frac{3}{2}}\\frac{3 \\left(4 t^{3} + t^{2} - 6 t - 9\\right)}{\\sqrt{16 t^{2} + 5}}\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539160} {:answer/id #uuid "a543efd7-e7a5-5a36-9b77-3946d717a988" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{\\frac{3}{2}}3 \\left(16 t^{2} + 5\\right) \\left(4 t^{3} + t^{2} - 6 t - 9\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539176}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a9fac12-ab2b-5bd8-ac50-2e5e3c1a9b49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\int_{1}^{\\frac{3}{2}}3 \\sqrt{16 t^{2} + 5} \\left(4 t^{3} + t^{2} - 6 t - 9\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539148} :answer-field/id #uuid "4103d898-eeb8-573e-bb32-54b6347b1152" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539143}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fcd80b68-90ad-5381-82bb-4a395bb5f45b" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\int_C 3 x y - 3 z^{2}\\,\\mathrm{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is parametrized by $r(t)=\\langle 2 t^{2}, 2 t + 1, t + 3\\rangle$ for $1\\le t\\le \\frac{3}{2}$?" :question/worked-solution "Since $r(t)=\\langle 2 t^{2}, 2 t + 1, t + 3\\rangle,$ then along the curve we have $x=2 t^{2}$, $y=2 t + 1$, $z=t + 3$.\n\nComputing $r'(t)$ and $\\|r'(t)\\|,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r'(t)&=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}t}\\,k \\\\ &= \\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2 t^{2})i+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2 t + 1)j+\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(t + 3)k \\\\ &= (4 t)\\,i+(2)\\,j+(1)\\,k \\\\ \\|r'(t)\\|&=\\sqrt{(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2+(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2+(\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}t})^2} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{(4 t)^2+(2)^2+(1)^2} \\\\ &= \\sqrt{16 t^{2} + 5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=3 x y - 3 z^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t))&=3\\left((2 t^{2})(2 t + 1)-(t + 3)^2\\right) \\\\ &= 3\\left(4t^3+(2)t^2-\\left(1t^2+(6)t+9\\right)\\right) \\\\ &= 12 t^{3} + 3 t^{2} - 18 t - 27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C f(x,y,z)\\,\\mathrm{d}s&=\\int_{1}^{\\frac{3}{2}} f(r(t))\\,\\|r'(t)\\|\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= \\int_{1}^{\\frac{3}{2}}(12 t^{3} + 3 t^{2} - 18 t - 27)(\\sqrt{16 t^{2} + 5})\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &= \\int_{1}^{\\frac{3}{2}}3 \\sqrt{16 t^{2} + 5} \\left(4 t^{3} + t^{2} - 6 t - 9\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539208 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "411d88e5-d8e0-59e2-9232-75d02c67dc6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{5} & \\frac{5}{4} & - \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ - \\frac{1}{4} & \\frac{5}{4} & - \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539226} {:answer/id #uuid "6a95c24c-3950-52f9-95dc-6fd2562407a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{9}{25} & - \\frac{3}{4} & \\frac{3}{10} \\\\ - \\frac{1}{4} & \\frac{5}{4} & - \\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539237} {:answer/id #uuid "71cd9849-777d-523c-9920-ab59ee979d28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{9}{25} & \\frac{3}{4} & - \\frac{3}{10} \\\