\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,v=xy\n$$\n\n \ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 9,\\,2\\le v\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}} & \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ y & \\text{ }\\text{ }x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}})}=-\\frac{1}{3u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(xy)^{2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}\\cdot |\\frac{1}{3u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{9}\\frac{{v}^{2}}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{4}{v}^{2}[{\\int }_{1}^{9}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u]\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{4}{v}^{2}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|\\,{|}_{1}^{9}]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{4}{v}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡9-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{3}{\\int }_{2}^{4}{v}^{2}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3}\\,{|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{9}\\cdot (64-8) \\\\  & =\\frac{112\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539380 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1760086a-7c09-5e8c-b8a1-c75d9fe9a51b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539359} {:answer/id #uuid "6a996faf-b917-5a49-b738-11415367209b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- 28 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539373} {:answer/id #uuid "7ea0e4fa-607a-52bf-a6d1-09e1f76b57f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539377} {:answer/id #uuid "cf2b0b28-1110-5655-97fd-3599b3b30adf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "84 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539385} {:answer/id #uuid "d44bf487-a72f-5688-b79f-679c5c9843de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "85 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539386}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ea0e4fa-607a-52bf-a6d1-09e1f76b57f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539377} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0c9be7b-466c-5bfe-beef-e661d5145178" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539389}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b880e9be-152d-5343-be07-baad51f74777" :question/problem "Using the change of variables $u=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}$ and $v=xy,$ evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(xy)^{2}\\,dx\\,dy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 16{x}^{2},\\,1\\le xy\\le 4\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,v=xy\n$$\n\n \ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 16,\\,1\\le v\\le 4\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}} & \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ y & \\text{ }\\text{ }x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}})}=-\\frac{1}{3u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(xy)^{2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}\\cdot |\\frac{1}{3u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{16}\\frac{{v}^{2}}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{v}^{2}[{\\int }_{1}^{16}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u]\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{v}^{2}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|\\,{|}_{1}^{16}]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{v}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡16-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{3}{\\int }_{1}^{4}{v}^{2}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3}\\,{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{9}\\cdot (64-1) \\\\  & =14\\operatorname{ln}⁡4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539387 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c8e42dd-1135-55d6-baea-5eaf5e395812" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{182 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539362} {:answer/id #uuid "5775d9e0-17da-5f4b-97d4-d8327fe13f79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{671 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539368} {:answer/id #uuid "bee804f0-fe47-54f2-82b3-1ab225bbaa16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539381} {:answer/id #uuid "c8511436-e484-5251-a621-0417962a64ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{182 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539383} {:answer/id #uuid "f7a1ff94-668d-56c9-ab15-77c13bfdd79a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{182 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539391}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c8e42dd-1135-55d6-baea-5eaf5e395812" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{182 \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539362} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a87ecd3-6574-5e98-aac6-eaeb2a2a41af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539361}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dc1cd88b-9881-56a3-9ef5-53708f7c4b33" :question/problem "Using the change of variables $u=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}$ and $v=xy,$ evaluate the double integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(xy)^{2}\\,dx\\,dy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 4{x}^{2},\\,5\\le xy\\le 6\\}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,v=xy\n$$\n\n \ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 4,\\,5\\le v\\le 6\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2y}{{x}^{3}} & \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ y & \\text{ }\\text{ }x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{3y}{{x}^{2}})}=-\\frac{1}{3u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(xy)^{2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{v}^{2}\\cdot |\\frac{1}{3u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{5}^{6}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{{v}^{2}}{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{5}^{6}{v}^{2}[{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u]\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{5}^{6}{v}^{2}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|\\,{|}_{1}^{4}]dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{5}^{6}{v}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3}{\\int }_{5}^{6}{v}^{2}\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}{v}^{3}\\,{|}_{5}^{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{9}\\cdot (216-125) \\\\  & =\\frac{182\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539398 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10b62b53-1b76-5884-8cf6-e623e6a2f279" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89.3\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539394} {:answer/id #uuid "1d6d3790-da14-581b-85b9-f63924341cef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89.8\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539400} {:answer/id #uuid "49ad23b9-5753-5821-bf10-fcf46d63b245" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539414} {:answer/id #uuid "4e0087ee-6487-5333-81b9-0c1398c381ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89.6\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539416} {:answer/id #uuid "8e62ca35-4a8a-52bf-a4f8-7c4ea42efb76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539433}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d6d3790-da14-581b-85b9-f63924341cef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "89.8\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539400} :answer-field/id #uuid "b807e3c0-f519-5770-bffe-d3aeea20ea64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539442}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a0728d4-a0d3-515f-805e-eb1a7d3c019c" :question/problem "A disinfectant reservoir in a water treatment facility can hold up to $180\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $60\\,\\text{gal}$ of disinfectant solution with $10\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved disinfectant. A more concentrated solution with concentration $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $25\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $20\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which disinfectant enters the reservoir minus the rate at which disinfectant leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which disinfectant enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of disinfectant enters the reservoir at a rate of $25\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *disinfectant* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (25\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (25\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =12.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which disinfectant exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which disinfectant leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $60\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $25\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $20\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=60+25t-20t=5(12+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{5(12+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $20\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *disinfectant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(20\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{5(12+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{5(12+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{20Q}{5(12+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{12+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =12.5-\\frac{4Q}{12+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{12+t}\\,Q=12.5.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 4/(12+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|12+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(12+t)^{4}} \\\\  & =(12+t)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(12+t)^{4}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(12+t)^{4}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{12+t}\\,Q) & =(12+t)^{4}\\cdot 12.5 \\\\ (12+t)^{4}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+4(12+t)^{3}Q & =12.5(12+t)^{4} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(12+t)^{4}) & =12.5(12+t)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(12+t)^{4})\\,dt & =\\int 12.5(12+t)^{4}\\,dt \\\\ Q(12+t)^{4} & =12.5\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}(12+t)^{5}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =2.5(12+t)+\\frac{C}{(12+t)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $10\\,\\text{lb}$ of disinfectant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=10,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =10 \\\\ 2.5(12+0)+\\frac{C}{(12+0)^{4}} & =10 \\\\ 30+\\frac{C}{(12)^{4}} & =10 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{12}^{4}} & =-20 \\\\ C & =-20(12)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =2.5(12+t)-\\frac{20(12)^{4}}{(12+t)^{4}} \\\\  & =2.5(12+t)-20{(\\frac{12}{12+t})}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $180\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=180$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =180 \\\\ 60+25{t}_{o}-20{t}_{o} & =180 \\\\ 60+5{t}_{o} & =180 \\\\ 5{t}_{o} & =120 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =24\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(24) & =2.5(12+24)-20{(\\frac{12}{12+24})}^{4} \\\\  & =90-20{(\\frac{12}{36})}^{4} \\\\  & ≈89.8\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539401 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3544b068-279b-57e6-9f38-0c9eaa23d33f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "235.5\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539406} {:answer/id #uuid "69907d51-3bf1-5b65-bbb2-ae831146ccb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "222.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539422} {:answer/id #uuid "872c8957-d06f-5407-86a5-616dbaa82761" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "117.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539430} {:answer/id #uuid "d8fcf795-dfb5-51ba-9ea4-76d781abc0a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "246.4\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539450} {:answer/id #uuid "e82e736f-6d11-5a2d-83f2-a27bd4f7db0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "294.4\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539454}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3544b068-279b-57e6-9f38-0c9eaa23d33f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "235.5\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539406} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f10b853-d565-5f3f-8bc1-e376bfffca18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539417}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "21bb65fc-94da-5d56-9b2a-2fe8f762fdb9" :question/problem "A disinfectant reservoir in a water treatment facility can hold up to $368\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $300\\,\\text{gal}$ of disinfectant solution with $160\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved disinfectant. A more concentrated solution with concentration $0.8\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $20\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which disinfectant enters the reservoir minus the rate at which disinfectant leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which disinfectant enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.8\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of disinfectant enters the reservoir at a rate of $20\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *disinfectant* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.8\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.8\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =16\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which disinfectant exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which disinfectant leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $300\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $20\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=300+20t-12t=8(37.5+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{8(37.5+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *disinfectant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(12\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{8(37.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{8(37.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12Q}{8(37.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.5Q}{37.5+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =16-\\frac{1.5Q}{37.5+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1.5}{37.5+t}\\,Q=16.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 1.5/(37.5+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{1.5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|37.5+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(37.5+t)^{1.5}} \\\\  & =(37.5+t)^{1.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(37.5+t)^{1.5}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(37.5+t)^{1.5}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1.5}{37.5+t}\\,Q) & =(37.5+t)^{1.5}\\cdot 16 \\\\ (37.5+t)^{1.5}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+1.5(37.5+t)^{0.5}Q & =16(37.5+t)^{1.5} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(37.5+t)^{1.5}) & =16(37.5+t)^{1.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(37.5+t)^{1.5})\\,dt & =\\int 16(37.5+t)^{1.5}\\,dt \\\\ Q(37.5+t)^{1.5} & =16\\cdot \\frac{1}{2.5}(37.5+t)^{2.5}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =6.4(37.5+t)+\\frac{C}{(37.5+t)^{1.5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $160\\,\\text{lb}$ of disinfectant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=160,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =160 \\\\ 6.4(37.5+0)+\\frac{C}{(37.5+0)^{1.5}} & =160 \\\\ 240+\\frac{C}{(37.5)^{1.5}} & =160 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{37.5}^{1.5}} & =-80 \\\\ C & =-80(37.5)^{1.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =6.4(37.5+t)-\\frac{80(37.5)^{1.5}}{(37.5+t)^{1.5}} \\\\  & =6.4(37.5+t)-80{(\\frac{37.5}{37.5+t})}^{1.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $368\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=368$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =368 \\\\ 300+20{t}_{o}-12{t}_{o} & =368 \\\\ 300+8{t}_{o} & =368 \\\\ 8{t}_{o} & =68 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =8.5\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of disinfectant in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(8.5) & =6.4(37.5+8.5)-80{(\\frac{37.5}{37.5+8.5})}^{1.5} \\\\  & =294.4-80{(\\frac{37.5}{46})}^{1.5} \\\\  & ≈235.5\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539408 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09aa09e5-f47d-5621-9398-dc8978b0d87a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "316.3\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539393} {:answer/id #uuid "3c471766-6b4e-5806-89be-2166ca930256" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "328.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539409} {:answer/id #uuid "89c88c07-7e92-5645-a2bb-e6beb66e7182" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "301.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539431} {:answer/id #uuid "b9a76597-8ded-5bde-a591-93a2815617ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "287.6\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539443} {:answer/id #uuid "d57c8381-a0fe-5352-bcb4-fdc5674d137c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "265.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539448}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89c88c07-7e92-5645-a2bb-e6beb66e7182" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "301.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539431} :answer-field/id #uuid "b68e3566-38f2-5496-a173-8df05cbc7a77" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3a39de0b-a8fb-57f4-97a8-3f35b38b481a" :question/problem "A saline reservoir for a medical research lab can hold up to $492\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution. Initially, it contains $213\\,\\text{gal}$ of saline solution with $\\frac{142}{3}\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved salt. A more concentrated saline solution with concentration $\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $15\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $9\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the reservoir minus the rate at which salt leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of salt enters the reservoir at a rate of $15\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =10\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which salt leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $213\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $15\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $9\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=213+15t-9t=6(35.5+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of salt in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{6(35.5+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $9\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(9\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{6(35.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(9\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{6(35.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9Q}{6(35.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.5Q}{35.5+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =10-\\frac{1.5Q}{35.5+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1.5}{35.5+t}\\,Q=10.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 1.5/(35.5+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{1.5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|35.5+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(35.5+t)^{1.5}} \\\\  & =(35.5+t)^{1.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(35.5+t)^{1.5}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(35.5+t)^{1.5}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1.5}{35.5+t}\\,Q) & =(35.5+t)^{1.5}\\cdot 10 \\\\ (35.5+t)^{1.5}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+1.5(35.5+t)^{0.5}Q & =10(35.5+t)^{1.5} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(35.5+t)^{1.5}) & =10(35.5+t)^{1.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(35.5+t)^{1.5})\\,dt & =\\int 10(35.5+t)^{1.5}\\,dt \\\\ Q(35.5+t)^{1.5} & =10\\cdot \\frac{1}{2.5}(35.5+t)^{2.5}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =4(35.5+t)+\\frac{C}{(35.5+t)^{1.5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $\\frac{142}{3}\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=\\frac{142}{3},$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =\\frac{142}{3} \\\\ 4(35.5+0)+\\frac{C}{(35.5+0)^{1.5}} & =\\frac{142}{3} \\\\ 142+\\frac{C}{(35.5)^{1.5}} & =\\frac{142}{3} \\\\ \\frac{C}{(35.5)^{1.5}} & =-\\frac{284}{3} \\\\ C & =-\\frac{284}{3}(35.5)^{1.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =4(35.5+t)-\\frac{\\frac{284}{3}(35.5)^{1.5}}{(35.5+t)^{1.5}} \\\\  & =4(35.5+t)-\\frac{284}{3}{(\\frac{35.5}{35.5+t})}^{1.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $492\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=492$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =492 \\\\ 213+15{t}_{o}-9{t}_{o} & =492 \\\\ 213+6{t}_{o} & =492 \\\\ 6{t}_{o} & =279 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =46.5\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(46.5) & =4(35.5+46.5)-\\frac{284}{3}{(\\frac{35.5}{35.5+46.5})}^{1.5} \\\\  & =328-\\frac{284}{3}{(\\frac{35.5}{82})}^{1.5} \\\\  & ≈301.0\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539423 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42b4b74e-a664-52ce-a195-21a3d605fb31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539411} {:answer/id #uuid "71401fcb-b5f8-5b4c-8bf5-f003372af32c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "39.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539424} {:answer/id #uuid "989c22ac-092f-5f6b-a7b9-e236e2ba3d64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "39.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539434} {:answer/id #uuid "a796dcf6-c12a-5697-9493-d0728388bc96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "38.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539437} {:answer/id #uuid "cf523ecc-0427-56f9-a3d5-e87e43240325" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "39.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539447}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf523ecc-0427-56f9-a3d5-e87e43240325" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "39.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539447} :answer-field/id #uuid "22f5cfc2-660c-58a7-af4f-432fa13dacdf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539402}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6991e06d-da9f-57bf-8c9b-3cc0e133e514" :question/problem "A dye mixing tank at a textile factory can hold up to $80\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $32\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution with $4\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved dye. A more concentrated dye solution with concentration $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of dye in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of dye in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which dye enters the tank minus the rate at which dye leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which dye enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of dye enters the tank at a rate of $8\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *dye* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (8\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (8\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =4\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which dye exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which dye leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $32\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid; solution enters at $8\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $6\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=32+8t-6t=2(16+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of dye in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{2(16+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $6\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *dye* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(6\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{2(16+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(6\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{2(16+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{6Q}{2(16+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3Q}{16+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =4-\\frac{3Q}{16+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{16+t}\\,Q=4.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 3/(16+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|16+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(16+t)^{3}} \\\\  & =(16+t)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(16+t)^{3}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(16+t)^{3}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{16+t}\\,Q) & =(16+t)^{3}\\cdot 4 \\\\ (16+t)^{3}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+3(16+t)^{2}Q & =4(16+t)^{3} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(16+t)^{3}) & =4(16+t)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(16+t)^{3})\\,dt & =\\int 4(16+t)^{3}\\,dt \\\\ Q(16+t)^{3} & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}(16+t)^{4}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =1(16+t)+\\frac{C}{(16+t)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $4\\,\\text{lb}$ of dye. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=4,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =4 \\\\ 1(16+0)+\\frac{C}{(16+0)^{3}} & =4 \\\\ 16+\\frac{C}{(16)^{3}} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{C}{(16)^{3}} & =-12 \\\\ C & =-12(16)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =1(16+t)-\\frac{12(16)^{3}}{(16+t)^{3}} \\\\  & =1(16+t)-12{(\\frac{16}{16+t})}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $80\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=80$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =80 \\\\ 32+8{t}_{o}-6{t}_{o} & =80 \\\\ 32+2{t}_{o} & =80 \\\\ 2{t}_{o} & =48 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =24\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of dye in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(24) & =1(16+24)-12{(\\frac{16}{16+24})}^{3} \\\\  & =40-12{(\\frac{16}{40})}^{3} \\\\  & ≈39.2\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539425 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1accc569-7a13-58f0-bb4e-fe4d837666e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30.5\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539399} {:answer/id #uuid "4c421d3c-a021-523f-b9ba-63f0c8410839" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21.5\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539415} {:answer/id #uuid "b2e8af6d-9653-5a1a-9535-1b2052824104" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "33.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539440} {:answer/id #uuid "d8aad8df-e1a6-5036-96dc-1c9eea79d2f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "41.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539449} {:answer/id #uuid "e4ac5ae2-38a9-5b58-b64a-b2b42879cc90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "37.8\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539452}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2e8af6d-9653-5a1a-9535-1b2052824104" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "33.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539440} :answer-field/id #uuid "b03249a1-4a5a-5426-a9d8-a51d09a0bcc1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539439}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7375207b-1d18-5bc8-88aa-4e960cad1491" :question/problem "A dye mixing tank at a textile factory can hold up to $82\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $33\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution with $\\frac{33}{8}\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved dye. A more concentrated dye solution with concentration $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the tank at a rate of $3\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $1\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of dye in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of dye in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which dye enters the tank minus the rate at which dye leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which dye enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of dye enters the tank at a rate of $3\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *dye* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (3\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (3\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =1.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which dye exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which dye leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $33\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid; solution enters at $3\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $1\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=33+3t-1t=2(16.5+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of dye in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{2(16.5+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $1\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *dye* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(1\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{2(16.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(1\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{2(16.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1Q}{2(16.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.5Q}{16.5+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =1.5-\\frac{0.5Q}{16.5+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{0.5}{16.5+t}\\,Q=1.5.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 0.5/(16.5+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{0.5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|16.5+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(16.5+t)^{0.5}} \\\\  & =(16.5+t)^{0.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(16.5+t)^{0.5}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(16.5+t)^{0.5}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{0.5}{16.5+t}\\,Q) & =(16.5+t)^{0.5}\\cdot 1.5 \\\\ (16.5+t)^{0.5}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+0.5(16.5+t)^{-0.5}Q & =1.5(16.5+t)^{0.5} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(16.5+t)^{0.5}) & =1.5(16.5+t)^{0.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(16.5+t)^{0.5})\\,dt & =\\int 1.5(16.5+t)^{0.5}\\,dt \\\\ Q(16.5+t)^{0.5} & =1.5\\cdot \\frac{1}{1.5}(16.5+t)^{1.5}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =1(16.5+t)+\\frac{C}{(16.5+t)^{0.5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $\\frac{33}{8}\\,\\text{lb}$ of dye. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=\\frac{33}{8},$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =\\frac{33}{8} \\\\ 1(16.5+0)+\\frac{C}{(16.5+0)^{0.5}} & =\\frac{33}{8} \\\\ 16.5+\\frac{C}{(16.5)^{0.5}} & =\\frac{33}{8} \\\\ \\frac{C}{(16.5)^{0.5}} & =-\\frac{99}{8} \\\\ C & =-\\frac{99}{8}(16.5)^{0.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =1(16.5+t)-\\frac{\\frac{99}{8}(16.5)^{0.5}}{(16.5+t)^{0.5}} \\\\  & =1(16.5+t)-\\frac{99}{8}{(\\frac{16.5}{16.5+t})}^{0.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $82\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=82$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =82 \\\\ 33+3{t}_{o}-1{t}_{o} & =82 \\\\ 33+2{t}_{o} & =82 \\\\ 2{t}_{o} & =49 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =24.5\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of dye in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(24.5) & =1(16.5+24.5)-\\frac{99}{8}{(\\frac{16.5}{16.5+24.5})}^{0.5} \\\\  & =41-\\frac{99}{8}{(\\frac{16.5}{41})}^{0.5} \\\\  & ≈33.1\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539438 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19329e8b-f3ba-516f-af71-d4b1d311b395" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "233.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539396} {:answer/id #uuid "3ebc62f3-c951-51bd-a0c0-b13a63587c9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "193.6\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539410} {:answer/id #uuid "8e496fc1-51c0-50fa-b05e-7b8545cfbf6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "243.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539432} {:answer/id #uuid "ec958c6a-8b57-50b9-9e6f-b329672750e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "220.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539455} {:answer/id #uuid "fed2300e-a029-550d-98c0-c43563de994e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "209.4\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539461}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec958c6a-8b57-50b9-9e6f-b329672750e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "220.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539455} :answer-field/id #uuid "36a6811d-be8c-5fac-8293-80fb147bef39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539407}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a7b8493b-92bf-5e7a-80a8-38bdcc3f94e7" :question/problem "A nutrient tank for a hydroponic greenhouse can hold up to $608gal$ of solution. Initially, it contains $276gal$ of nutrient solution with $36.8lb$ of dissolved nutrient. A more concentrated solution with concentration $0.4lb/gal$ flows into the tank at a rate of $20gal/min$, while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $12gal/min$. Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of nutrient in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of nutrient in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which nutrient enters the tank minus the rate at which nutrient leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which nutrient enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.4\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of nutrient enters the tank at a rate of $20\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *nutrient* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.4\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.4\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =8\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which nutrient exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which nutrient leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of solution in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $276\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $20\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=276+20t-12t=8(34.5+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of nutrient in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{8(34.5+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *nutrient* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(12\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{8(34.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{8(34.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12Q}{8(34.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1.5Q}{34.5+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =8-\\frac{1.5Q}{34.5+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1.5}{34.5+t}\\,Q=8.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 1.5/(34.5+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{1.5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|34.5+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(34.5+t)^{1.5}} \\\\  & =(34.5+t)^{1.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(34.5+t)^{1.5}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(34.5+t)^{1.5}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{1.5}{34.5+t}\\,Q) & =(34.5+t)^{1.5}\\cdot 8 \\\\ (34.5+t)^{1.5}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+1.5(34.5+t)^{0.5}Q & =8(34.5+t)^{1.5} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(34.5+t)^{1.5}) & =8(34.5+t)^{1.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(34.5+t)^{1.5})\\,dt & =\\int 8(34.5+t)^{1.5}\\,dt \\\\ Q(34.5+t)^{1.5} & =8\\cdot \\frac{1}{2.5}(34.5+t)^{2.5}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =3.2(34.5+t)+\\frac{C}{(34.5+t)^{1.5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $36.8\\,\\text{lb}$ of nutrient. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=36.8,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =36.8 \\\\ 3.2(34.5+0)+\\frac{C}{(34.5+0)^{1.5}} & =36.8 \\\\ 110.4+\\frac{C}{(34.5)^{1.5}} & =36.8 \\\\ \\frac{C}{(34.5)^{1.5}} & =-73.6 \\\\ C & =-73.6(34.5)^{1.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =3.2(34.5+t)-\\frac{73.6(34.5)^{1.5}}{(34.5+t)^{1.5}} \\\\  & =3.2(34.5+t)-73.6{(\\frac{34.5}{34.5+t})}^{1.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $608\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=608$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =608 \\\\ 276+20{t}_{o}-12{t}_{o} & =608 \\\\ 276+8{t}_{o} & =608 \\\\ 8{t}_{o} & =332 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =41.5\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of nutrient in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(41.5) & =3.2(34.5+41.5)-73.6{(\\frac{34.5}{34.5+41.5})}^{1.5} \\\\  & =243.2-73.6{(\\frac{34.5}{76})}^{1.5} \\\\  & ≈220.7\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539444 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "195e6da9-9d93-5a3f-abbd-e60df2db40ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "288.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539397} {:answer/id #uuid "3070ff1a-7364-514c-8463-27955639a9a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "287.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539404} {:answer/id #uuid "c36c1a8a-ce2f-5284-b361-d99daa4298d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "290.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539445} {:answer/id #uuid "c7bef1a7-bd82-52c0-a027-9770a4051e62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "289.6\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539446} {:answer/id #uuid "f2f09433-dc8c-51ad-bba5-d5f7876a82dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "287.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539458}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "195e6da9-9d93-5a3f-abbd-e60df2db40ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "288.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539397} :answer-field/id #uuid "86cc63e8-6dde-540e-9e8a-53412c30e26f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539429}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bd6eb638-b03a-52f5-961d-00f47eebd890" :question/problem "A saline reservoir for a medical research lab can hold up to $290\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution. Initially, it contains $110\\,\\text{gal}$ of saline solution with $55\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved salt. A more concentrated saline solution with concentration $1.0\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $25\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $20\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the reservoir minus the rate at which salt leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $1.0\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of salt enters the reservoir at a rate of $25\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(1.0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (25\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(1.0\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (25\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =25\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which salt leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $110\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $25\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $20\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=110+25t-20t=5(22+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of salt in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{5(22+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $20\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(20\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{5(22+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(20\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{5(22+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{20Q}{5(22+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{22+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =25-\\frac{4Q}{22+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{22+t}\\,Q=25.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 4/(22+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|22+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(22+t)^{4}} \\\\  & =(22+t)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(22+t)^{4}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(22+t)^{4}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{22+t}\\,Q) & =(22+t)^{4}\\cdot 25 \\\\ (22+t)^{4}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+4(22+t)^{3}Q & =25(22+t)^{4} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(22+t)^{4}) & =25(22+t)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(22+t)^{4})\\,dt & =\\int 25(22+t)^{4}\\,dt \\\\ Q(22+t)^{4} & =25\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}(22+t)^{5}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =5(22+t)+\\frac{C}{(22+t)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $55\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=55,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =55 \\\\ 5(22+0)+\\frac{C}{(22+0)^{4}} & =55 \\\\ 110+\\frac{C}{(22)^{4}} & =55 \\\\ \\frac{C}{(22)^{4}} & =-55 \\\\ C & =-55(22)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =5(22+t)-\\frac{55(22)^{4}}{(22+t)^{4}} \\\\  & =5(22+t)-55{(\\frac{22}{22+t})}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $290\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=290$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =290 \\\\ 110+25{t}_{o}-20{t}_{o} & =290 \\\\ 110+5{t}_{o} & =290 \\\\ 5{t}_{o} & =180 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =36\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(36) & =5(22+36)-55{(\\frac{22}{22+36})}^{4} \\\\  & =290-55{(\\frac{22}{58})}^{4} \\\\  & ≈288.9\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539451 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33657fab-2d53-58a0-98a5-5356a1b0e53a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "226.4\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539405} {:answer/id #uuid "481acaa8-6906-5974-ae76-54c5df18a291" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "226.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539412} {:answer/id #uuid "49a7b172-e749-59a4-86e1-727a1b566135" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "227.5\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539413} {:answer/id #uuid "a6d6b5ed-3b6b-5ab9-afd8-dc8db825fe1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "228.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539436} {:answer/id #uuid "ecee97e7-7992-52dc-9300-131e135e7abf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "227.8\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539456}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49a7b172-e749-59a4-86e1-727a1b566135" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "227.5\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539413} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6be6c4b-dcbc-5ac0-a692-8e76768f9b9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539453}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "df8520ff-a1e4-578f-8153-7bfeb8954209" :question/problem "A nutrient tank for a hydroponic greenhouse can hold up to $342gal$ of solution. Initially, it contains $117gal$ of nutrient solution with $39lb$ of dissolved nutrient. A more concentrated solution with concentration $\\frac{2}{3}lb/gal$ flows into the tank at a rate of $45gal/min$, while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $36gal/min$. Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of nutrient in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of nutrient in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which nutrient enters the tank minus the rate at which nutrient leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which nutrient enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of nutrient enters the tank at a rate of $45\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *nutrient* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (45\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (45\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =30\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which nutrient exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which nutrient leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of solution in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $117\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $45\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $36\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=117+45t-36t=9(13+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of nutrient in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{9(13+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $36\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *nutrient* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(36\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{9(13+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(36\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{9(13+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{36Q}{9(13+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4Q}{13+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =30-\\frac{4Q}{13+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{13+t}\\,Q=30.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 4/(13+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡|13+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(13+t)^{4}} \\\\  & =(13+t)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(13+t)^{4}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(13+t)^{4}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{4}{13+t}\\,Q) & =(13+t)^{4}\\cdot 30 \\\\ (13+t)^{4}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+4(13+t)^{3}Q & =30(13+t)^{4} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(13+t)^{4}) & =30(13+t)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(13+t)^{4})\\,dt & =\\int 30(13+t)^{4}\\,dt \\\\ Q(13+t)^{4} & =30\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}(13+t)^{5}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =6(13+t)+\\frac{C}{(13+t)^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $39\\,\\text{lb}$ of nutrient. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=39,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =39 \\\\ 6(13+0)+\\frac{C}{(13+0)^{4}} & =39 \\\\ 78+\\frac{C}{(13)^{4}} & =39 \\\\ \\frac{C}{(13)^{4}} & =-39 \\\\ C & =-39(13)^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =6(13+t)-\\frac{39(13)^{4}}{(13+t)^{4}} \\\\  & =6(13+t)-39{(\\frac{13}{13+t})}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $342\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=342$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =342 \\\\ 117+45{t}_{o}-36{t}_{o} & =342 \\\\ 117+9{t}_{o} & =342 \\\\ 9{t}_{o} & =225 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =25\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of nutrient in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(25) & =6(13+25)-39{(\\frac{13}{13+25})}^{4} \\\\  & =228-39{(\\frac{13}{38})}^{4} \\\\  & ≈227.5\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539460 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59522728-6ff5-53b4-81e0-6fc27377f260" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "139.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539419} {:answer/id #uuid "5cc28afa-a913-5db8-b93f-4620f9101e19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539421} {:answer/id #uuid "7393c7e7-e7db-5dae-8dbd-5c124fce7375" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "153.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539426} {:answer/id #uuid "7b517137-5fec-5646-b994-ef492f5bc290" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "135.6\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539428} {:answer/id #uuid "f63a9d0e-447c-57d4-a11e-5dba82466c9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "130.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539459}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b517137-5fec-5646-b994-ef492f5bc290" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "135.6\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539428} :answer-field/id #uuid "5063c8db-660b-5ca6-90ed-32587c3a7e76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539418}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fbffe068-df49-5949-a93c-dd6aa67cd689" :question/problem "A saline reservoir for a medical research lab can hold up to $306\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution. Initially, it contains $234\\,\\text{gal}$ of saline solution with $78\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved salt. A more concentrated saline solution with concentration $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $24\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $18\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the reservoir minus the rate at which salt leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.5\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of salt enters the reservoir at a rate of $24\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (24\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (24\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =12\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which salt leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $234\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $24\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $18\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=234+24t-18t=6(39+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of salt in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{6(39+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $18\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(18\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{6(39+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(18\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{6(39+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{18Q}{6(39+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3Q}{39+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =12-\\frac{3Q}{39+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{39+t}\\,Q=12.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 3/(39+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|39+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(39+t)^{3}} \\\\  & =(39+t)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(39+t)^{3}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(39+t)^{3}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{3}{39+t}\\,Q) & =(39+t)^{3}\\cdot 12 \\\\ (39+t)^{3}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+3(39+t)^{2}Q & =12(39+t)^{3} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(39+t)^{3}) & =12(39+t)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(39+t)^{3})\\,dt & =\\int 12(39+t)^{3}\\,dt \\\\ Q(39+t)^{3} & =12\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}(39+t)^{4}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =3(39+t)+\\frac{C}{(39+t)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $78\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=78,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =78 \\\\ 3(39+0)+\\frac{C}{(39+0)^{3}} & =78 \\\\ 117+\\frac{C}{(39)^{3}} & =78 \\\\ \\frac{C}{(39)^{3}} & =-39 \\\\ C & =-39(39)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =3(39+t)-\\frac{39(39)^{3}}{(39+t)^{3}} \\\\  & =3(39+t)-39{(\\frac{39}{39+t})}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $306\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=306$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =306 \\\\ 234+24{t}_{o}-18{t}_{o} & =306 \\\\ 234+6{t}_{o} & =306 \\\\ 6{t}_{o} & =72 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =12\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(12) & =3(39+12)-39{(\\frac{39}{39+12})}^{3} \\\\  & =153-39{(\\frac{39}{51})}^{3} \\\\  & ≈135.6\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186539462 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17d1e1a0-3c64-5ef9-9dbe-759c9e661398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "386.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539395} {:answer/id #uuid "2bf04547-57c3-53c4-840a-33e618532e81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "284.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539403} {:answer/id #uuid "5acc0bd1-19cd-5dac-ac79-38ebff3903c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "588.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539420} {:answer/id #uuid "797eee29-2209-55c5-906c-e8f2d054d896" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "458.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539427} {:answer/id #uuid "a62cbaf9-65b9-5cb1-860f-98a4438ffabe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "273.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539435}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17d1e1a0-3c64-5ef9-9dbe-759c9e661398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "386.0\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539395} :answer-field/id #uuid "f243c2a4-f8d5-5f1f-93bf-3f804ba469a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539457}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ff0fe97f-83da-58e7-9a2a-1f41369f6103" :question/problem "A saline reservoir for a medical research lab can hold up to $490\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution. Initially, it contains $315\\,\\text{gal}$ of saline solution with $126\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved salt. A more concentrated saline solution with concentration $1.2\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the reservoir at a rate of $15\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $5\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of salt in the reservoir at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which salt enters the reservoir minus the rate at which salt leaves the reservoir.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which salt enters the reservoir.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $1.2\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of salt enters the reservoir at a rate of $15\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *salt* enters the reservoir is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(1.2\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(1.2\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (15\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =18\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which salt exits the reservoir.\n\nTo find the rate at which salt leaves the reservoir, we must first determine the volume of solution in the reservoir at time $t.$\n\nThe reservoir initially contains $315\\,\\text{gal}$ of solution; solution enters at $15\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $5\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of solution in the reservoir after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=315+15t-5t=10(31.5+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the reservoir is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of salt in the reservoir, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{10(31.5+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $5\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *salt* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(5\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{10(31.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(5\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{10(31.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5Q}{10(31.5+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.5Q}{31.5+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =18-\\frac{0.5Q}{31.5+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{0.5}{31.5+t}\\,Q=18.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 0.5/(31.5+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{0.5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|31.5+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(31.5+t)^{0.5}} \\\\  & =(31.5+t)^{0.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(31.5+t)^{0.5}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(31.5+t)^{0.5}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{0.5}{31.5+t}\\,Q) & =(31.5+t)^{0.5}\\cdot 18 \\\\ (31.5+t)^{0.5}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+0.5(31.5+t)^{-0.5}Q & =18(31.5+t)^{0.5} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(31.5+t)^{0.5}) & =18(31.5+t)^{0.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(31.5+t)^{0.5})\\,dt & =\\int 18(31.5+t)^{0.5}\\,dt \\\\ Q(31.5+t)^{0.5} & =18\\cdot \\frac{1}{1.5}(31.5+t)^{1.5}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =12(31.5+t)+\\frac{C}{(31.5+t)^{0.5}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the reservoir initially contains $126\\,\\text{lb}$ of salt. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=126,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =126 \\\\ 12(31.5+0)+\\frac{C}{(31.5+0)^{0.5}} & =126 \\\\ 378+\\frac{C}{(31.5)^{0.5}} & =126 \\\\ \\frac{C}{(31.5)^{0.5}} & =-252 \\\\ C & =-252(31.5)^{0.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =12(31.5+t)-\\frac{252(31.5)^{0.5}}{(31.5+t)^{0.5}} \\\\  & =12(31.5+t)-252{(\\frac{31.5}{31.5+t})}^{0.5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the reservoir begins to overflow. The reservoir has a capacity of $490\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=490$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =490 \\\\ 315+15{t}_{o}-5{t}_{o} & =490 \\\\ 315+10{t}_{o} & =490 \\\\ 10{t}_{o} & =175 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =17.5\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of salt in the reservoir at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(17.5) & =12(31.5+17.5)-252{(\\frac{31.5}{31.5+17.5})}^{0.5} \\\\  & =588-252{(\\frac{31.5}{49})}^{0.5} \\\\  & ≈386.0\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540025 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e540eb0-8cac-5747-b4e7-b7ccd316406a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539509} {:answer/id #uuid "19977cc3-abaa-5675-a5a2-9c0cb09a3a33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539533} {:answer/id #uuid "3f2d1a27-b068-55ec-bdb4-71d7924738b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539592} {:answer/id #uuid "b1c54165-081f-572b-8aab-18d7b5b1b30e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539782} {:answer/id #uuid "d0b025ce-348b-58ed-829c-e108dbb26644" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539837}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "19977cc3-abaa-5675-a5a2-9c0cb09a3a33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539533} :answer-field/id #uuid "eefd7b42-cc63-5201-b954-24eada1a35de" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540012}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1404f19f-22e0-51b4-954a-73312901eafa" :question/math-academy-id "q-107142" :question/problem "![](images/92/920aca572a3b6fc828d6c37246b348aa2b017d1bc4e022ea06c12d0fa09ad6ee.png)\n\nConsider the finite region bounded by the curves $x=(1-y)^{2}$ and $y=\\sqrt{1-x},$ as shown above. Calculate the volume of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, we write the second function in terms $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt{1-x} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1-x \\\\ x & =1-{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let $f(y)=1-{y}^{2}$ and $g(y)=(1-y)^{2}.$\n\nWe start by determining the points where the curves intersect by solving the following equation for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(y) & =g(y) \\\\ 1-{y}^{2} & =(1-y)^{2} \\\\ 1-{y}^{2} & =1-2y+{y}^{2} \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-2y & =0 \\\\ 2y(y-1) & =0 \\\\ y & =0,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to integrate from $y=0$ to $y=1.$ \n\nNow, let's draw our usual horizontal arrow through the region to determine the left and right functions.\n\n![](images/02/02e6b4204b1edf682dd332e28830dc84aa0e41d3728c66442d777f6fe9daccfc.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the left function $x=g(y)=(1-y)^{2},$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the right function $x=f(y)=1-{y}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{c}^{d}y([\\text{right}]-[\\text{left}])\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y([1-{y}^{2}]-[(1-y)^{2}])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y([1-{y}^{2}]-[1-2y+{y}^{2}])\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}y(2y-2{y}^{2})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{y}^{2}-2{y}^{3})\\text{d}y \\\\  & =4\\pi ({\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{3}\\text{d}y) \\\\  & =4\\pi ({\\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}|}_{0}^{1}-{\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}|}_{0}^{1}) \\\\  & =4\\pi ((\\frac{1}{3}-0)-(\\frac{1}{4}-0)) \\\\  & =4\\pi (\\frac{1}{12}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540048 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "263e9c79-2b5d-5fc6-ae10-bec804651dad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}30 \\\\ 42\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539553} {:answer/id #uuid "5e6bef22-fee1-53f1-8d0f-2529ad295f2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}30 & 42\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539639} {:answer/id #uuid "85ee42d0-6b46-5068-8546-0b6d45122f68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}21 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539700} {:answer/id #uuid "a2df55cc-4d93-55a0-9838-4a5a0b419174" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}58 & 26\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539752} {:answer/id #uuid "ae374fd7-8d71-5715-bb99-e0fa0ee5417f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}58 \\\\ 26\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539776}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae374fd7-8d71-5715-bb99-e0fa0ee5417f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}58 \\\\ 26\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539776} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4ae1559-7267-51e9-bbdf-d599082e8f60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539977}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3f634cb0-d91f-55a0-8036-9bf4e6795f5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-269525" :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2}\\to R$ defined by $L(x)=(Ax-2b)^{T}(Ax-2b)$ where \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{x}L(x)$ at $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]?$" :question/worked-solution "We can solve this problem by applying the chain rule for gradients.\n\nLet $y=Ax-2b.$ Then, $L(x)={y}^{T}y,$ and\n\n$$\n{∇}_{x}L={(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}\\cdot {(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y})}^{T}.\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}({y}^{T}y)=2{y}^{T}\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(Ax-2b)=A,\n$$\n\nwe have that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x})}^{T}\\cdot {(\\frac{\\text{d}L}{\\text{d}y})}^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{x}L & ={A}^{T}\\cdot 2y \\\\  & =2\\cdot {A}^{T}(Ax-2b) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{T}([\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]([\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}58 \\\\ 26\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540062 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58e41124-d27e-5bf8-a849-55991f19b628" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539631} {:answer/id #uuid "b1098335-0f3d-532a-9ba1-59d86a55f4e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539778} {:answer/id #uuid "bd65e325-f1b8-5c87-9662-6c18254e66f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539807} {:answer/id #uuid "f74d244e-220f-5aa8-ba66-446e22db924d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539897} {:answer/id #uuid "fadee215-c360-5052-9061-2ac46dc7f6ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539902}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58e41124-d27e-5bf8-a849-55991f19b628" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539631} :answer-field/id #uuid "db152ebb-c302-5112-8c45-9948cd14ca8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540002}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "789a361b-1deb-51ca-88a0-26707116d922" :question/math-academy-id "q-65381" :question/problem "![](images/e9/e980e9415d52338c90eb5c167a32b02815237b1554f9aea06f831e88db6f3777.png)\n\n$\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that $cot$ is the reciprocal of $tan.$ So first we need to compute tangent:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{adjacent}}=\\frac{PR}{QR}=\\frac{3}{QR}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the length $QR,$ we can use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}QR & =\\sqrt{P{Q}^{2}-P{R}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(3\\sqrt{5})^{2}-{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{45-9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{6}=\\frac{1}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{-1}=2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540074 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f66b5f8-e76f-5f77-8f96-098a448df87a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539542} {:answer/id #uuid "22e0ee74-658c-52fe-a2b1-2df54d380881" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539547} {:answer/id #uuid "38ae8a7e-8879-58a2-b77d-2f1efa7ea909" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539585} {:answer/id #uuid "9579f35d-0a62-56c0-87be-1dce6c45f688" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539727} {:answer/id #uuid "fdf065d6-21d7-507f-b607-cc7b49b70a85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539911}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9579f35d-0a62-56c0-87be-1dce6c45f688" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539727} :answer-field/id #uuid "42169019-4c90-5f92-9ed4-0e9272ff36d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539949}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9728a2a0-30c7-53d5-836b-39033ca5b896" :question/math-academy-id "q-122902" :question/problem "![](images/a9/a9003434f721485b5ee3eb2ed417d0c4c68514b98060f6149bc974270e55411a.png)\n\n$\\operatorname{csc}⁡P=$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that $csc$ is the reciprocal of $sin.$ So first we need to compute sine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡P=\\frac{\\text{opposite}}{\\text{hypotenuse}}=\\frac{QR}{PR}=\\frac{QR}{15}\n$$\n\nTo find the length $QR,$ we can use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}QR & =\\sqrt{P{R}^{2}-P{Q}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{15}^{2}-{9}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{225-81} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{144} \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡P=\\frac{QR}{15}=\\frac{12}{15}=\\frac{4}{5},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡P={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{-1}=\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540100 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d56b3b6-d2ee-5a6a-ac79-f166c602aad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539536} {:answer/id #uuid "5bbc6a18-26ef-5ee7-a216-046a93109cdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539634} {:answer/id #uuid "684d8919-d630-53ec-be88-da83985b9cfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539650} {:answer/id #uuid "adf72623-7732-5460-b0b6-5cc1011adb27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539775} {:answer/id #uuid "f71975b1-e674-5e94-a227-f47c16e02797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539894}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f71975b1-e674-5e94-a227-f47c16e02797" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539894} :answer-field/id #uuid "746714c2-cc0f-5a43-9606-b934d8793273" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186539966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dba16e2f-f755-5c6f-b25b-801d348b9282" :question/math-academy-id "q-67435" :question/problem "Solve the equation $-\\frac{4}{x-2}=\\frac{1}{x-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n- $x=2$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=1$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{x-2}{4}=\\frac{x-1}{1}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator, $4.$ The equation becomes\n\n$$\n-\\frac{x-2}{4}=\\frac{4x-4}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4)\\cdot (\\frac{x-2}{-4}) & =(-4)\\cdot \\frac{4x-4}{4} \\\\ x-2 & =-4x+4 \\\\ x+4x & =4+2 \\\\ 5x & =6 \\\\ (\\frac{1}{5})\\cdot (5x) & =(\\frac{1}{5})\\cdot (6) \\\\ (\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}})\\cdot (\\cancel{5}x) & =(\\frac{1}{5})\\cdot (6) \\\\ x & =\\frac{6}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540456 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c322d1d5-fa93-5b4d-95c0-830b54df1e15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{31}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540494}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c322d1d5-fa93-5b4d-95c0-830b54df1e15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{31}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540494} :answer-field/id #uuid "672d067c-a7f8-5d32-b7dd-2a9c164ead57" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540466} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "837c5d7e-5c7a-55c0-a1dd-2c6a1f5a7c94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540472}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "837c5d7e-5c7a-55c0-a1dd-2c6a1f5a7c94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540472} :answer-field/id #uuid "71bcb1f3-c0ec-5af1-9ecb-8c82c317c35c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540468} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "674927f3-92ff-5d26-a00d-cb0a1f3ad243" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{19}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540467}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "674927f3-92ff-5d26-a00d-cb0a1f3ad243" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{19}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540467} :answer-field/id #uuid "8fdcf7e5-dd52-509d-8532-451e7e4957a8" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540479}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3c34ce35-aef6-5c54-826e-13e18dd80841" :question/problem "Let $u=⟨3,\\frac{7}{2},-2⟩$ and $v=⟨\\frac{1}{2},-1,5⟩.$ Find $u\\times v.$\n\n$u\\times v=⟨${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$,$\n{{field-3}}$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & \\frac{7}{2} & -2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & -1 & 5\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{2} & -2 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 5\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}3 & \\frac{7}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & -1\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =((\\frac{7}{2})(5)-(-2)(-1))i-((3)(5)-(-2)(\\frac{1}{2}))j+((3)(-1)-(\\frac{7}{2})(\\frac{1}{2}))k \\\\  & =(\\frac{35}{2}-(2))i-(15-(-1))j+(-3-(\\frac{7}{4}))k \\\\  & =\\frac{31}{2}i-16j-\\frac{19}{4}k \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{31}{2},-16,-\\frac{19}{4}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540471 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "083370b1-ba72-5090-86d4-3a98f815ca42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-2,-\\frac{79}{10},\\frac{17}{5}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540442} {:answer/id #uuid "3aa1026d-b21a-538c-9efb-fc6dfba4a3e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{79}{10},-\\frac{17}{5},2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540455} {:answer/id #uuid "83d427ec-a05b-57c1-b3a1-26cb9989f5f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨12,-\\frac{121}{10},\\frac{23}{5}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540473} {:answer/id #uuid "ad06b859-0b73-5512-aaa2-eb46767b7e40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,\\frac{79}{10},-\\frac{17}{5}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540486} {:answer/id #uuid "c03d7484-43b2-53d9-a04d-756366cee69e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,-\\frac{79}{10},-\\frac{17}{5}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540490}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad06b859-0b73-5512-aaa2-eb46767b7e40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨2,\\frac{79}{10},-\\frac{17}{5}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540486} :answer-field/id #uuid "43a5d5e9-93a6-5b25-801f-3e73c6bcbbe5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540458}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8258bec8-eb13-513a-bb18-127d6cd09d35" :question/problem "Let $u=⟨\\frac{3}{5},2,5⟩$ and $v=⟨2,1,\\frac{7}{2}⟩.$ Find $u\\times v.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{3}{5} & 2 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 1 & \\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & 5 \\\\ 2 & \\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =((2)(\\frac{7}{2})-(5)(1))i-((\\frac{3}{5})(\\frac{7}{2})-(5)(2))j+((\\frac{3}{5})(1)-(2)(2))k \\\\  & =(7-(5))i-(\\frac{21}{10}-(10))j+(\\frac{3}{5}-(4))k \\\\  & =2i+\\frac{79}{10}j-\\frac{17}{5}k \\\\  & =⟨2,\\frac{79}{10},-\\frac{17}{5}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540482 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a7523b92-e0fa-5100-89df-7dae96eaf50d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540485}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7523b92-e0fa-5100-89df-7dae96eaf50d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540485} :answer-field/id #uuid "03642954-db1f-5b56-baba-c60918de52e5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540441} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "154386c8-e60b-57cd-89b8-ad30585cdf0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540444}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "154386c8-e60b-57cd-89b8-ad30585cdf0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540444} :answer-field/id #uuid "b20ad369-865c-5616-8eee-c0227b79e977" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540488} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43b45ff0-de68-500f-ae65-ebd7d951b4f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540459}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "43b45ff0-de68-500f-ae65-ebd7d951b4f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540459} :answer-field/id #uuid "fda0061c-c650-5039-a061-eeffb1ce737c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540509}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a0e7674f-c68b-5ba3-8599-974d4b1beac2" :question/problem "Let $u=4i+2j-\\frac{4}{3}k$ and $v=-4i+4j+\\frac{1}{3}k.$ Find $u\\times v.$\n\n$u\\times v=$\n\n{{field-1}}$\\,i\\,+\\,$\n{{field-2}}$\\,j\\,+\\,$\n{{field-3}}$\\,k$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 4 & 2 & -\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ -4 & 4 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & -\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ 4 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}4 & -\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ -4 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -4 & 4\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =((2)(\\frac{1}{3})-(-\\frac{4}{3})(4))i-((4)(\\frac{1}{3})-(-\\frac{4}{3})(-4))j+((4)(4)-(2)(-4))k \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{3}-(-\\frac{16}{3}))i-(\\frac{4}{3}-(\\frac{16}{3}))j+(16-(-8))k \\\\  & =6i+4j+24k \\\\  & =⟨6,4,24⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540489 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4af1c12e-b138-529d-9912-81429586885e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{3}{4},-\\frac{25}{4},\\frac{7}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540461} {:answer/id #uuid "66be531f-acfc-566c-952f-5c82b25ac6f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{3}{4},-\\frac{25}{4},-\\frac{7}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540465} {:answer/id #uuid "a4e38e1f-ca4e-5edd-80e9-760d59c37bd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{25}{4},-\\frac{7}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540484} {:answer/id #uuid "c555375d-64f4-5eb0-baea-1a6328f3c1dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{25}{4},-\\frac{7}{2},\\frac{3}{4}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540495} {:answer/id #uuid "ecb70fa6-35b2-5b98-9141-cdcd799d17f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨-\\frac{13}{4},-\\frac{15}{4},\\frac{3}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540506}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4e38e1f-ca4e-5edd-80e9-760d59c37bd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "⟨\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{25}{4},-\\frac{7}{2}⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540484} :answer-field/id #uuid "98d0eee8-8d34-5f0a-b375-50554cb9ecc1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540481}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bf9484b3-9081-5c6e-811b-eb353ca9f662" :question/problem "Let $u=⟨-1,-1,-2⟩$ and $v=⟨-\\frac{5}{2},1,\\frac{5}{4}⟩.$ Find $u\\times v.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & 1 & \\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & \\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & \\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & 1\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =((-1)(\\frac{5}{4})-(-2)(1))i-((-1)(\\frac{5}{4})-(-2)(-\\frac{5}{2}))j+((-1)(1)-(-1)(-\\frac{5}{2}))k \\\\  & =(-\\frac{5}{4}-(-2))i-(-\\frac{5}{4}-(5))j+(-1-(\\frac{5}{2}))k \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}i+\\frac{25}{4}j-\\frac{7}{2}k \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{4},\\frac{25}{4},-\\frac{7}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186540499 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "240d3498-fa54-5157-88f1-3818e0422e4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16i-\\frac{37}{10}j+\\frac{59}{5}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540450} {:answer/id #uuid "27ea1601-7690-5806-b2f8-3fec36a8db4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8i+\\frac{107}{10}j-\\frac{11}{5}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540453} {:answer/id #uuid "85523c49-77fc-5c77-be0e-f3d65c058fea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{37}{10}i+\\frac{59}{5}j+16k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540475} {:answer/id #uuid "c8659aa4-9b80-5734-ad39-99d9e9608d60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16i-\\frac{37}{10}j-\\frac{59}{5}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540498} {:answer/id #uuid "da155be7-0d97-5290-b3c8-388435680f44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16i+\\frac{37}{10}j+\\frac{59}{5}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da155be7-0d97-5290-b3c8-388435680f44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16i+\\frac{37}{10}j+\\frac{59}{5}k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540502} :answer-field/id #uuid "c298c57a-7dab-5682-bf12-994f4328b57a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540493}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c879baa4-8bcb-559c-9780-708721266a3e" :question/problem "Let $u=-\\frac{6}{5}i+2j+k$ and $v=-\\frac{7}{2}i-4j+6k.$ Find $u\\times v.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the determinant formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\times v & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -\\frac{6}{5} & 2 & 1 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2} & -4 & 6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{6}{5} & 1 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2} & 6\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{6}{5} & 2 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2} & -4\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =((2)(6)-(1)(-4))i-((-\\frac{6}{5})(6)-(1)(-\\frac{7}{2}))j+((-\\frac{6}{5})(-4)-(2)(-\\frac{7}{2}))k \\\\  & =(12-(-4))i-(-\\frac{36}{5}-(-\\frac{7}{2}))j+(\\frac{24}{5}-(-7))k \\\\  & =16i+\\frac{37}{10}j+\\frac{59}{5}k \\\\  & =⟨16,\\frac{37}{10},\\frac{59}{5}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541058 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72d5bd5e-7472-57dd-bbfd-f86c424a9071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(t^2-2)(t^2+25)}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540725}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72d5bd5e-7472-57dd-bbfd-f86c424a9071" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(t^2-2)(t^2+25)}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540725} :answer-field/id #uuid "796d2edf-72cd-5edb-a250-8f0040849fed" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540991}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "08e75bed-6305-557a-ac6f-4a738a0fec12" :question/math-academy-id "q-133287" :question/problem "The eagle's path traces a curve $C$ in the $xy$-plane. Suppose that the eagle is at the point $P$ on $C$ at time $t.$ Expressed in terms of $t,$ what function gives the slope of the tangent to $C$ at $P?$\n\nThe slope of the tangent to $C$ at time $t$ equals$\\,$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The slope of the tangent to $C$ equals $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$ To calculate this, we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)}.\n$$\n\nIn parts 1 and 2, we found\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{5}{25+{t}^{2}},\\,{y}^{′}(t)={t}^{2}-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{t}^{2}-2}{(\\frac{5}{25+{t}^{2}})}=\\frac{1}{5}({t}^{2}-2)({t}^{2}+25).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541061 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2bfa8ff-2170-5808-88ce-f923ae4b22b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540799}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2bfa8ff-2170-5808-88ce-f923ae4b22b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540799} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a634dcb-2ecf-54f4-96f1-be7f5d63e9a4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540963}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0dfc8af8-b45c-5404-a021-aa0515c46816" :question/math-academy-id "q-352254" :question/problem "Consider the finite region bounded by the curve $y=-4+5x-{x}^{2}$ and the $x$-axis. Calculate the volume $V$ of the solid generated when this region is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine the points where the curve intersects the $x$-axis by solving the following equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4+5x-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (x-1)(x-4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solutions are $x=1$ and $x=4.$ So, we need to integrate from $x=1$ to $x=4.$  \n\nUsing the shell method, the volume of the solid can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =2\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}x\\,f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}x(-4+5x-{x}^{2})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{4}(-4x+5{x}^{2}-{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {(-2{x}^{2}+\\frac{5}{3}{x}^{3}-\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{4})|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =2\\pi ((-2({4}^{2})+\\frac{5}{3}({4}^{3})-\\frac{1}{4}({4}^{4}))-(-2+\\frac{5}{3}-\\frac{1}{4})) \\\\  & =2\\pi (\\frac{45}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{45\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541069 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f4b480b-46bc-5ad0-b2dc-179d612978aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-2s^2-2s-6}{s(s-1)(s+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540741}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f4b480b-46bc-5ad0-b2dc-179d612978aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-2s^2-2s-6}{s(s-1)(s+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540741} :answer-field/id #uuid "1abf10fb-79e5-50b9-8eca-b49f881c098e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "24075bbd-fad1-5e70-847c-3b96960e39c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-333072" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{2}(t)+3,\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$ In terms of the parameter $s,$ the solution of the new system is\n\n${X}_{1}(s)=$ {{field-1}},$\\,$\n${X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{3}{s(s+1)}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}L\\{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t)\\} \\\\ L\\{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{-{x}_{2}(t)+3\\}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}sL\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-{x}_{1}(0)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-2L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\} \\\\ sL\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}-{x}_{2}(0)=-L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}+3L\\{1\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)+2={X}_{1}(s)-2{X}_{2}(s) \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)-0=-{X}_{2}(s)+\\frac{3}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\},$ and we have used the fact that $L\\{1\\}=\\frac{1}{s}.$\n\nNow, solving the second equation for ${X}_{2}(s),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{2}(s) & =-{X}_{2}(s)+\\frac{3}{s} \\\\ (s+1){X}_{2}(s) & =\\frac{3}{s} \\\\ {X}_{2}(s) & =\\frac{3}{s(s+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this in the first equation and solving for ${X}_{1}(s)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)+2 & ={X}_{1}(s)-2{X}_{2}(s) \\\\ (s-1){X}_{1}(s) & =-2-2{X}_{2}(s) \\\\ (s-1){X}_{1}(s) & =-2-2(\\frac{3}{s(s+1)}) \\\\ (s-1){X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{-2s(s+1)-6}{s(s+1)} \\\\ (s-1){X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{-2({s}^{2}+s)-6}{s(s+1)} \\\\ (s-1){X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{-2{s}^{2}-2s-6}{s(s+1)} \\\\ {X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{-2{s}^{2}-2s-6}{s(s+1)(s-1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution of the new system in terms of parameter $s$ is \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{-2{s}^{2}-2s-6}{s(s-1)(s+1)},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{3}{s(s+1)}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541073 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63c2faca-0edb-5ef8-8326-5900825078b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\cdot {6}^{3/5}}{2}{x}^{11/5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540697} {:answer/id #uuid "73e207b9-91a9-5b1a-8a15-f29313c6b433" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{12/5}}{12}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540727} {:answer/id #uuid "da7a61db-d353-5a53-b318-1be353f60fe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{31}^{2/5}}{32}{x}^{7/5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540895} {:answer/id #uuid "ed9e9b62-8e00-5346-a13f-699ec44a7450" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{7}^{2/5}}{2}{x}^{7/5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540927} {:answer/id #uuid "fbfa7707-765d-5937-8a04-fb11b50efc05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{36}^{2/5}}{72}{x}^{12/5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540936}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73e207b9-91a9-5b1a-8a15-f29313c6b433" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{12/5}}{12}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540727} :answer-field/id #uuid "05367c1f-7c48-5001-bd7f-8cd94f9a1ee7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540944}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "31f8bbd7-44e6-58a8-9f8d-def7c80fe8a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-29652" :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{7}}?$" :question/worked-solution "We start by writing the root as a rational power, $\\sqrt[5]{{x}^{7}}={x}^{7/5}.$ So, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{7}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\sqrt[5]{{x}^{7}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {x}^{7/5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{7/5+1}}{(\\frac{7}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{12/5}}{(\\frac{12}{5})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{12/5}}{12}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{12/5}}{12}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{12/5}}{12})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{12}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{12/5})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{12}\\cdot \\frac{12}{5}{x}^{12/5-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{7/5}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541074 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "141b96db-d2b7-5c1e-ba1e-d4cec0dff378" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "136.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540562} {:answer/id #uuid "56903a87-08bb-5210-b21a-15bdc9bdba3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "135.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540671} {:answer/id #uuid "6eb90bb5-f5d6-5864-b41d-7da56b21a5e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "136.4\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540720} {:answer/id #uuid "bba99957-7e8d-5791-b9fd-9c58160a4cde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "135.7\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540843} {:answer/id #uuid "c0463d14-2b9e-5fba-b2c4-9802c27cf249" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "135.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540849}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "141b96db-d2b7-5c1e-ba1e-d4cec0dff378" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "136.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540562} :answer-field/id #uuid "464a658d-9140-58a9-ae22-53b8f7f33f64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540976}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "36a65a87-c8d0-5bbf-adbe-0789f4e08164" :question/math-academy-id "q-336500" :question/problem "A syrup mixing tank at a bottling plant can hold up to $220\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $120\\,\\text{gal}$ of syrup solution with $24\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved sugar. A more concentrated sugar solution with concentration $0.7\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $8\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of sugar in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which sugar enters the tank minus the rate at which sugar leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which sugar enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $0.7\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of sugar enters the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *sugar* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(0.7\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(0.7\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =8.4\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which sugar exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which sugar leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $120\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid; solution enters at $12\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $8\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=120+12t-8t=4(30+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of sugar in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{4(30+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $8\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *sugar* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(8\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{4(30+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(8\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{4(30+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8Q}{4(30+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{30+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =8.4-\\frac{2Q}{30+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{30+t}\\,Q=8.4.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(30+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|30+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(30+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(30+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(30+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(30+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{30+t}\\,Q) & =(30+t)^{2}\\cdot 8.4 \\\\ (30+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(30+t)Q & =8.4(30+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30+t)^{2}) & =8.4(30+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 8.4(30+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(30+t)^{2} & =8.4\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(30+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =2.8(30+t)+\\frac{C}{(30+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $24\\,\\text{lb}$ of sugar. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=24,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =24 \\\\ 2.8(30+0)+\\frac{C}{(30+0)^{2}} & =24 \\\\ 84+\\frac{C}{(30)^{2}} & =24 \\\\ \\frac{C}{(30)^{2}} & =-60 \\\\ C & =-60(30)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =2.8(30+t)-\\frac{60(30)^{2}}{(30+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =2.8(30+t)-60{(\\frac{30}{30+t})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $220\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=220$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =220 \\\\ 120+12{t}_{o}-8{t}_{o} & =220 \\\\ 120+4{t}_{o} & =220 \\\\ 4{t}_{o} & =100 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =25\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of sugar in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(25) & =2.8(30+25)-60{(\\frac{30}{30+25})}^{2} \\\\  & =154-60{(\\frac{30}{55})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈136.1\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541076 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "222b62ab-1bfb-546b-aab5-03561e3c701f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540586} {:answer/id #uuid "262dc74b-263d-5d80-9193-e12e8b9113bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540595} {:answer/id #uuid "3fc77f0f-f146-5ca7-b666-ad8985b69724" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540635} {:answer/id #uuid "e0610972-c22b-5f94-966b-2d4ed2ec3bd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540910} {:answer/id #uuid "e423743e-6949-5391-8f90-a809db7277ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540914}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "262dc74b-263d-5d80-9193-e12e8b9113bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540595} :answer-field/id #uuid "030979c4-2ad6-5121-addd-f709ea3b08d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540943}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "40216ba0-427a-539f-9353-a0236ab249d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-85235" :question/problem "![](images/fd/fd812d1d33801a3ce8368feca0e7d6b87e02bcfab5d904d9b59172d95d0204e5.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/e0/e087efb9eb18158005722dd6628cc8f19b22a2b33cad8f67205b8ea398e62c54.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{6})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{11}{36}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{25}{36}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{5}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{6}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{5}{6})}=\\frac{6}{5}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541084 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "074012c5-6ca1-5495-8009-0d0c255d3ee8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540534} {:answer/id #uuid "1bc800b9-5ce9-51df-9646-ad28b4b5f5dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540575} {:answer/id #uuid "81f7e7be-4c3c-5db4-9e89-a897730e6bd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{155}{32}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540748} {:answer/id #uuid "afc9329d-23eb-511f-aef5-7c8e3284646b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{41}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540823} {:answer/id #uuid "d18a0d10-9bd4-5e8e-9023-0b2590d9b92f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540882}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "074012c5-6ca1-5495-8009-0d0c255d3ee8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{15}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540534} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a192929-90b9-5ade-b716-7719dbfc5bbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540967}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "59606949-3c87-521e-a2c2-60c040472354" :question/math-academy-id "q-109956" :question/problem "For two independent random variables $X$ and $Y,$ we have $\\text{Var}[X]=16$ and $\\text{Var}[Y]=6.$ Find $\\text{Var}[\\frac{3}{8}X+\\frac{1}{2}Y].$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are independent variables, and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+bY]={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+{b}^{2}\\text{Var}[Y].\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given data into the formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[\\frac{3}{8}X+\\frac{1}{2}Y]={(\\frac{3}{8})}^{2}\\cdot 16+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}\\cdot 6=\\frac{15}{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541085 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2aecc83e-62f9-5570-b3ec-23bbc0d4c666" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{2{e}^{t}+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540602} {:answer/id #uuid "434933b7-c938-5b99-9c27-958c4224b195" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540643} {:answer/id #uuid "602e3020-e08a-5741-9ce8-35913e0c5082" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{t/2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540692} {:answer/id #uuid "66c00b74-0490-5e7d-8d8b-1cd2cf6001b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540703} {:answer/id #uuid "cc74ab54-9cb6-55f6-a841-1af50094d0ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{2t}\\sqrt{{e}^{t/2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540867}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66c00b74-0490-5e7d-8d8b-1cd2cf6001b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540703} :answer-field/id #uuid "09078c01-f245-5447-88a1-e69ec53cbb38" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540946}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5b66c4d7-d9b1-5cb6-840d-18a9a3471ea0" :question/math-academy-id "q-138966" :question/problem "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx={e}^{t},\\,y=2{e}^{t/2},\\,t\\in [0,4].\n$$\n\nWhat integral gives the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis?" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=4.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{t/2})={e}^{t/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}y\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}2{e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{{({e}^{t})}^{2}+{({e}^{t/2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}({e}^{t}+1)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{t/2}\\cdot {e}^{t/2}\\cdot \\sqrt{{e}^{t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{4}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541088 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1053c1f5-d753-5d4f-9ed6-23685e190240" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1053c1f5-d753-5d4f-9ed6-23685e190240" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540557} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1265d9c-4abc-5c77-ab0f-534e48c095b2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541033}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "65741bd8-a817-5dbe-b425-cf51f2c41352" :question/math-academy-id "q-223688" :question/problem "The axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=(2x+5)(2x+k)$ is $x=-\\frac{9}{4}.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we will calculate the axis of symmetry in terms of $k.$ Then, since we know that the axis of symmetry is $x=-\\frac{9}{4},$ we will set our result equal to $-\\frac{9}{4}.$ \n\nFrom the equation $y=(2x+5)(2x+k),$ we find that the roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{k}{2}.$ \n\nTo find the axis of symmetry, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{5}{2}-\\frac{k}{2})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{5+k}{2})}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5+k}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the axis of symmetry is $x=-\\frac{9}{4}.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5+k}{4} & =-\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ 5+k & =9 \\\\ k & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541091 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10217f51-caf3-5dd4-96ac-8656fdc36c51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540556} {:answer/id #uuid "3bbc2cd9-05ee-5ab2-b7f8-ecea5083e238" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540626} {:answer/id #uuid "4655fb84-1019-5281-8d52-c7b7aa2b05c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540649} {:answer/id #uuid "80cb97b9-3cce-5ddf-a76a-626c77b83982" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540744} {:answer/id #uuid "bb0e0e46-2dfb-5983-8032-25be5dcb57b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540842}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80cb97b9-3cce-5ddf-a76a-626c77b83982" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540744} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b703d74-20ff-5a85-bdd0-120e273b7e1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540949}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "67eb7841-2530-5536-9fe7-7fe1cd1941b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-37897" :question/problem "![](images/1b/1b084e0ad229e935d70d751f0e0ddaacf5832017951a7ef8e334f27834662d5c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/57/5778175613309c2b4b8007291ad122b879de1cd409ad3513f3f786c8f4885927.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{5}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{4}{9}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nKnowing that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{2}{3})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541094 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41a9bbe8-dcee-5623-9122-ba87f8a7d390" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "176.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540640} {:answer/id #uuid "52c67f33-11db-51b1-9650-af1cf2900407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "175.8\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540668} {:answer/id #uuid "a20928a6-6ffa-5ddc-9782-9f840a1c74b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "176.5\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540798} {:answer/id #uuid "aca174a9-712f-5452-958f-e15b38456d2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "175.2\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540818} {:answer/id #uuid "d7682d19-2baf-59af-84e2-dbac5b3c49b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "176.9\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41a9bbe8-dcee-5623-9122-ba87f8a7d390" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "176.1\\,\\text{lb}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540640} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2e476d3-efaf-5ec7-9cea-2c3e7d2d49f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541048}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6c55e9d5-5244-5391-84ab-9850b2ba8152" :question/math-academy-id "q-336508" :question/problem "A wastewater treatment tank at a chemical plant can hold up to $180\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid. Initially, it contains $120\\,\\text{gal}$ of wastewater with $40\\,\\text{lb}$ of dissolved contaminant. A more concentrated wastewater stream with concentration $1.25\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ flows into the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min},$ while the completely mixed solution drains out at a rate of $8\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ Rounded to one decimal place, find the amount of contaminant in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow." :question/worked-solution "Since $Q$ is the amount of contaminant in the tank at time $t,$ the derivative \n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}\n$$\n\ndescribes how this amount changes with respect to time $t,$ in minutes. This is equal to the rate at which contaminant enters the tank minus the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}\n$$\n\nNote that the units of $\\frac{dQ}{dt},{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}$ and ${Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′}$ are $\\text{lb}/\\text{min}.$ Now, let's compute the required rates of change in steps.\n\n**Step 1**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant enters the tank.\n\nFirst, note that a solution containing $1.25\\,\\text{lb/gal}$ of contaminant enters the tank at a rate of $12\\,\\text{gal/min}.$ So, the rate at which the *contaminant* enters the tank is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′} & =(1.25\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =(1.25\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}})\\cdot (12\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}}) \\\\  & =15\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Calculating the rate at which contaminant exits the tank.\n\nTo find the rate at which contaminant leaves the tank, we must first determine the volume of liquid in the tank at time $t.$\n\nThe tank initially contains $120\\,\\text{gal}$ of liquid; solution enters at $12\\,\\text{gal/min}$ and mixed solution leaves at $8\\,\\text{gal/min}.$\nAs a result, the amount of liquid in the tank after $t$ minutes is \n\n$$\nV(t)=120+12t-8t=4(30+t)\\,\\text{gal}.\n$$\n\nNote that $V(t)$ is an increasing function, so the tank is filling.\n\nSince $Q$ is the amount of contaminant in the tank, the concentration of the solution at time $t$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{Q}{4(30+t)}\\,\\text{lb/gal}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, as the completely mixed solution leaves at a rate of $8\\,\\text{gal/min},$ *contaminant* leaves at a rate of\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} & =(8\\,\\frac{\\text{gal}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{4(30+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{gal}}) \\\\  & =(8\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}{\\text{min}})\\cdot (\\frac{Q}{4(30+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\cancel{\\text{gal}}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8Q}{4(30+t)}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2Q}{30+t}\\,\\frac{\\text{lb}}{\\text{min}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Calculate the overall rate of change.\n\nThe rate of change equals the rate of entry minus the rate of exit. Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dQ}{dt} & ={Q}_{\\text{in}}^{′}-{Q}_{\\text{out}}^{′} \\\\  & =15-\\frac{2Q}{30+t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{30+t}\\,Q=15.\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Integrate the equation.\n\nThis is a first-order linear equation with variable coefficients. So we find the general solution using an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(t) & ={e}^{\\int 2/(30+t)\\,dt} \\\\  & ={e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|30+t|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(30+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =(30+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the differential equation by $I(t)=(30+t)^{2}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(30+t)^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{dQ}{dt}+\\frac{2}{30+t}\\,Q) & =(30+t)^{2}\\cdot 15 \\\\ (30+t)^{2}\\frac{dQ}{dt}+2(30+t)Q & =15(30+t)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30+t)^{2}) & =15(30+t)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dt}(Q(30+t)^{2})\\,dt & =\\int 15(30+t)^{2}\\,dt \\\\ Q(30+t)^{2} & =15\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}(30+t)^{3}+C \\\\ Q(t) & =5(30+t)+\\frac{C}{(30+t)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the tank initially contains $40\\,\\text{lb}$ of contaminant. So, we can apply the initial condition $Q(0)=40,$ and solve for $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(0) & =40 \\\\ 5(30+0)+\\frac{C}{(30+0)^{2}} & =40 \\\\ 150+\\frac{C}{(30)^{2}} & =40 \\\\ \\frac{C}{{30}^{2}} & =-110 \\\\ C & =-110(30)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following expression for $Q(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(t) & =5(30+t)-\\frac{110(30)^{2}}{(30+t)^{2}} \\\\  & =5(30+t)-110{(\\frac{30}{30+t})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let ${t}_{o}$ be the time when the tank begins to overflow. The tank has a capacity of $180\\,\\text{gal},$ so we can use the condition $V({t}_{o})=180$ to solve for ${t}_{o}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V({t}_{o}) & =180 \\\\ 120+12{t}_{o}-8{t}_{o} & =180 \\\\ 120+4{t}_{o} & =180 \\\\ 4{t}_{o} & =60 \\\\ {t}_{o} & =15\\,\\text{min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, rounded to one decimal place, the amount of contaminant in the tank at the moment it begins to overflow is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(15) & =5(30+15)-110{(\\frac{30}{30+15})}^{2} \\\\  & =225-110{(\\frac{30}{45})}^{2} \\\\  & ≈176.1\\,\\text{lb}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541096 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "beda0c51-788b-57b5-9c1d-b837a9961c32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{t^2+25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "beda0c51-788b-57b5-9c1d-b837a9961c32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{t^2+25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540846} :answer-field/id #uuid "a16e017f-d9d9-54ce-97a3-a549b27da1ca" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541011}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6e20ee7e-741b-5069-a51c-7de25cc32da7" :question/math-academy-id "q-133285" :question/problem "Calculate the horizontal velocity of the eagle as a function of time.\n\nThe horizontal velocity of the bird is$\\,$ \n{{field-1}}$\\,\\text{m/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal velocity of the eagle is given by ${x}^{′}(t),$ where \n\n$$\nx(t)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{t}{5}).\n$$\n\nThe formula for the derivative of arctangent is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t)=\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule and the formula above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{t}{5})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+(t/5)^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{t}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}/25}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{25+{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the horizontal velocity of the eagle is given by the function ${x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{5}{25+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{m/s}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541100 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9cc6edc-3d58-5b7d-be26-011da007a10e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{80}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9cc6edc-3d58-5b7d-be26-011da007a10e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{80}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540814} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5800f9f-a331-5cd2-b8d9-dac2799c0d71" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541013}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "742d438b-e3dc-52b0-a068-888e70c6be15" :question/math-academy-id "q-133290" :question/problem "At time $t=5\\,\\text{s}$ the eagle is located at a point on $C$ with $y$-coordinate equal to $\\frac{95}{3}\\,\\text{m}.$ Use the tangent line approximation to approximate the $y$-coordinate of the eagle's location when $t=\\frac{110}{23}\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n$y(\\frac{110}{23})≈$\n{{field-1}}$\\,\\text{m}$" :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation to $y(t)$ at the point $a=5$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(t) & =y(a)+{y}^{′}(a)(t-a) \\\\  & =y(5)+{y}^{′}(5)(t-5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $y(5)=\\frac{95}{3},$ and since ${y}^{′}(t)={t}^{2}-2,$ then ${y}^{′}(5)=25-2=23.$ \n\nSo, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(t) & =\\frac{95}{3}+23(t-5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $y(\\frac{110}{23})$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=\\frac{110}{23}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\frac{110}{23}) & ≈L(\\frac{110}{23}) \\\\  & =\\frac{95}{3}+23(\\frac{110}{23}-5) \\\\  & =\\frac{95}{3}+23(-\\frac{5}{23}) \\\\  & =\\frac{95}{3}-5 \\\\  & =\\frac{80}{3}\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541108 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c412eada-9cf7-59a5-905e-0f6c0063eb13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t^3}{3}-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c412eada-9cf7-59a5-905e-0f6c0063eb13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t^3}{3}-2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540856} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1f591a2-abaf-568b-95d2-b28ddb7d4b0c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541017}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "836e24be-b58e-500b-bb05-e9b2030ce5e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-133291" :question/problem "Find $y(t),$ the antiderivative of ${y}^{′}(t).$\n\n$y(t)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+\\,C$" :question/worked-solution "We know that ${y}^{′}(t)={t}^{2}-2.$\n\nApplying the sum rule, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {t}^{2}-2\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int {t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t-\\int 2\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int {t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t-2\\int {t}^{0}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{{t}^{2+1}}{2+1})-2t+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}-2t+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541114 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69712d4c-92b7-5f9d-abcd-ca7ad46c6567" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-s^2+10s+4}{s(s+1)(s+2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540707}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69712d4c-92b7-5f9d-abcd-ca7ad46c6567" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-s^2+10s+4}{s(s+1)(s+2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540707} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8de0228-5ef5-53a6-b77b-949275d4025a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541022}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9ca91093-1f1e-55da-a457-e3e1666bf899" :question/math-academy-id "q-333069" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-2{x}_{2}(t)+1,\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$ In terms of the parameter $s,$ the solution of the new system is\n\n${X}_{1}(s)=$ {{field-1}} $,\\,$\n${X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{3s+1}{s(s+2)}$ ." :question/worked-solution "First, we compute the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}L\\{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{-{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t)\\} \\\\ L\\{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{-2{x}_{2}(t)+1\\}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}sL\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-{x}_{1}(0)=-L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+4L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\} \\\\ sL\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}-{x}_{2}(0)=-2L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}+L\\{1\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)+1=-{X}_{1}(s)+4{X}_{2}(s) \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)-3=-2{X}_{2}(s)+\\frac{1}{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\},$ and we have used the fact that $L\\{1\\}=\\frac{1}{s}.$\n\nNow, solving the second equation for ${X}_{2}(s),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{2}(s)-3 & =-2{X}_{2}(s)+\\frac{1}{s} \\\\ (s+2){X}_{2}(s) & =3+\\frac{1}{s} \\\\ (s+2){X}_{2}(s) & =\\frac{3s+1}{s} \\\\ {X}_{2}(s) & =\\frac{3s+1}{s(s+2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this in the first equation and solving for ${X}_{1}(s)$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)+1 & =-{X}_{1}(s)+4{X}_{2}(s) \\\\ (s+1){X}_{1}(s) & =4{X}_{2}(s)-1 \\\\ (s+1){X}_{1}(s) & =4(\\frac{3s+1}{s(s+2)})-1 \\\\ (s+1){X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{4(3s+1)-s(s+2)}{s(s+2)} \\\\ (s+1){X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{12s+4-({s}^{2}+2s)}{s(s+2)} \\\\ (s+1){X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{-{s}^{2}+10s+4}{s(s+2)} \\\\ {X}_{1}(s) & =\\frac{-{s}^{2}+10s+4}{s(s+2)(s+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution of the new system in terms of parameter $s$ is \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{-{s}^{2}+10s+4}{s(s+1)(s+2)},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{3s+1}{s(s+2)}.\n$$"}]
